var tafsirData_21 = {
  "1": "\"people’s judgment has drawn near\" opens the chapter of the prophets, and the opening is a warning of nearness. The word for \"judgment\" is 'hisab,' the reckoning, the settling of accounts, and the verse says it has drawn near. The Arabic verb 'iqtaraba' means to come close, and the verse does not say the judgment is far in the future. It says it is approaching, the way an appointed meeting approaches as the days pass. To the people of Mecca who first heard this, and who thought the Day of Judgment was a distant legend, the verse said: the reckoning of all people is already on its way. The commentators note the shift in perspective: in the scale of the world's history, the Hour is near, because everything that has a fixed time is near to the one who is waiting for it, and every human being is waiting for it whether they know it or not.\n\nThe second half of the verse describes what the people are doing while it approaches: \"yet they are heedlessly turning away\". The word behind \"heedlessly\" is 'ghaflah,' the state of being absorbed in something else, and the phrase says they turn away while wrapped in their distractions. This is the exact picture the chapter will develop: the judgment approaches, and humanity plays on. The verse does not describe the judgment's horrors yet. It simply places the two facts side by side, the approaching reckoning and the turning away, and lets the gap between them do the warning. The chapter that follows will show what the heedless say about the message, what the message answers, and what the reckoning will actually bring, and every verse of it is spoken to people living inside that gap. The opening also names the audience of the whole chapter: people whose account is approaching. Every warning that follows, every story of a prophet and his people, is addressed to that condition, and the chapter will not let the listener forget it.",
  "2": "\"Whatever new reminder comes to them from their Lord\" describes the arrival of revelation, and the word \"whatever\" is the verse's way of saying it happens again and again. The reminders keep coming, each one fresh, each one from their Lord, and the response never changes: \"they only listen to it jokingly\". The Arabic phrase is 'istama'uhu wa hum yal'abun,' they listen while they play, and the picture is of a group hearing the Quran the way one hears background noise at a game, half-listening, nudging one another, smiling. The word \"only\" is the indictment: their listening produces nothing but mockery. The same scene appears elsewhere in the Quran in matching words: \"Whatever new reminder comes to them from the Most Compassionate, they always turn away from it.\"\n\nThe verse is an anatomy of a certain kind of rejection. These are not people who have never heard the message, and they are not people who have considered it and found it false. They are people who have made the hearing itself into entertainment. Mockery is their shield, because if the message can be turned into a joke, it never has to be taken seriously. The verse wants the reader to see the strategy for what it is. A joke is the cheapest way to dismiss a truth, and it works by never letting the truth get close enough to be examined. There is also a mercy hidden in the verse's first word. The reminders keep coming, and each one is new, because God does not give up on a people after one delivery. The same Lord who sent the first reminder keeps sending more, and the mockery is met with patience in the sending, which is the only patience that outlasts the mockery. The next verse will look inside these listeners and show what is actually happening behind the laughter, and the chapter will spend its length answering the jokes with the one thing mockery cannot survive: the truth, stated plainly and repeated.",
  "3": "\"with their hearts ˹totally˺ distracted\" completes the portrait the previous verse began, and it goes behind the laughter. The listeners are not just playing on the surface; the verse says their hearts are distracted, occupied with other things, and the word \"totally\" pushes the distraction to its full extent. The Arabic 'lahiyat' means hearts that are busy elsewhere, and the verse is the Quran's diagnosis of why the reminder does not reach them: the ear is free, but the heart is engaged somewhere else. Listening without the heart is not listening at all, and the verse explains the mockery of the previous verse with this single fact. Hearts that are full of the world have no room for the reminder, and so the reminder becomes a joke.\n\nThe verse then shows what such people do instead of reflecting: \"The evildoers would converse secretly\". The word for the secret conversation is 'najwa,' the whispered counsel, and its content is recorded: \"Is this ˹one˺ not human like yourselves? Would you fall for ˹this˺ witchcraft, even though you can ˹clearly˺ see?\" Their argument is twofold: the messenger is only human, and his message is witchcraft. The irony sits in the last words. They say they can clearly see, and the verse has just said their hearts are distracted. The people who claim the clearest vision are the ones whose hearts are closed, and the secret conversation, which they thought was hidden, is being read out in the open by the One who heard it. The verse also reveals the weakness inside their own argument. If the messenger is only human like them, then he is close enough to be examined, and his life is open to their eyes. The charge they thought was fatal is actually the invitation: here is a man among you, so look at him. And if it is witchcraft, then the proof of its falsehood should be easy to find, yet their only proof is to whisper about it in secret. The next verse gives the Prophet's answer, and it answers the whisper directly.",
  "4": "\"My Lord ˹fully˺ knows every word spoken in the heavens and the earth\" is the Prophet's answer to the secret conversation, and it is an answer that does not argue. He has just been told, in the previous verse, what the evildoers whisper among themselves, and his response is not to counter their points one by one. It is to point to the One who hears the whisper itself. The verse says God knows every word in the heavens and the earth, so the private conversation of the mockers is not private. It is heard, recorded, and answered, and the Prophet's calm comes from exactly this: the case is not being tried in their secret councils. It is being tried before the One who hears everything.\n\nThe verse then names the two names of God that match the situation: \"For He is the All-Hearing, All-Knowing\". The pairing is precise. They mocked with their words, and He is the All-Hearing; they hid their thoughts, and He is the All-Knowing. Both the spoken and the hidden are inside His knowledge, and the Prophet leaves them there. The answer is also a lesson in how a believer responds to slander and secret mockery. The natural reaction is to defend oneself against the whispers, but the verse models something higher: entrust the whispers to the One who hears them. The Prophet's confidence did not need to answer every accusation because he knew who was listening. The verse is one of the shortest in the chapter, and it carries one of its largest comforts: nothing said against the truth is ever said in secret. The verse also gives the believer a rule for dealing with secret mockery: do not chase the whisper, because the whisper is already heard. The One who knows every word in the heavens and the earth has the whole case in His hands, and the accused does not need to defend himself in the councils of the accusers. His defense is being prepared in a higher court.",
  "5": "\"This ˹Quran˺ is a set of confused dreams!\" is the first of the accusations the verse records, and the list that follows is itself an argument. The word for \"confused dreams\" is 'adghathu ahlam,' jumbled fragments of sleep, and the accusers mean that the Quran is a mixture of disjointed visions with no order and no source. Then the accusation shifts: \"No, he has fabricated it!\" If it is not dreams, it is a forgery, a book the Prophet made up. Then the accusation shifts again: \"No, he must be a poet!\" If it is not a forgery, it is poetry, beautiful words with no divine origin. The three accusations cancel each other out, and the verse records the cancellation. A man who cannot be accused of one thing is accused of three contradictory things, because the accusers are not describing what they found; they are reaching for anything that removes the need to believe.\n\nThe verse then records their demand: \"So let him bring us a ˹tangible˺ sign like those ˹prophets˺ sent before.\" They want a visible miracle of the kind earlier prophets brought, a staff or a she-camel, and the demand is the one the next verses will answer at length. The commentators note the deeper point of the verse: the very confusion of the accusations proves the truth was troubling them. People do not search for three different explanations of something that does not exist. They search for explanations when they have met something real and are trying to explain it away. The demand for a sign will be answered many times in this chapter, and the answer will come from their own history: the prophets they cite as examples all brought signs, and their peoples were destroyed for rejecting them. The sign is not what they lack; acceptance is. The verse preserves their words so that every reader can see the shape of a mind in flight from the truth, and the chapter will now turn to answer, with the stories of the prophets they themselves appealed to.",
  "6": "\"Not a ˹single˺ society We destroyed before them ever believed ˹after receiving the signs˺\" states a historical law, and the word \"single\" gives it the force of a complete record. The verse is answering the demand of the previous passage, where the disbelievers asked for a sign. It tells them to look at every nation that came before them, every people to whom signs were sent and who were then destroyed for their rejection: not one of them believed when the destruction arrived. The verse is not speculating; it is reading the whole ledger of history. Nations have been given signs, have mocked them, and have been destroyed, and the pattern has never once ended in a last-minute conversion that saved them. Belief under the falling punishment is not belief; it is terror, and it has never counted.\n\nThen the verse turns to its own audience with the question that makes the law personal: \"Will these ˹pagans˺ then believe?\" The question expects one answer, and the answer is no. The pagans of Mecca are not a new kind of humanity. They are the same kind as every nation before them, asking the same question, making the same demand, and heading toward the same end unless they break the pattern while there is still time. The verse is the Quran's way of saying: do not flatter yourselves into thinking you will be the exception. The only people who ever escaped the pattern were the ones who believed before the punishment, not the ones who asked for a sign and promised to believe after it. The question hangs over the whole chapter, and every story that follows is another entry in the ledger this verse has just read aloud. The verse is also a mercy in its bluntness. A warning that leaves room for the hope of being the exception is no warning at all, and the verse closes the exception off on purpose, because the only hope it wants to leave is the hope of believing now, before the pattern can repeat.",
  "7": "\"We did not send ˹messengers˺ before you ˹O Prophet˺ except mere men inspired by Us\" answers the accusation that a messenger should not be an ordinary human being, and the answer is history itself. Every messenger God ever sent was a man. The word \"mere men\" is the Quran's way of closing the matter: no angels as public messengers, no supernatural beings walking the streets, but human men, the one difference being the phrase \"inspired by Us\", the revelation that set them apart. The accusers thought the humanity of the Prophet was the flaw in his claim. The verse says it is the pattern of all prophecy, and the pattern was never an accident. A messenger who is human can be watched, questioned, and imitated, and his life becomes part of the message itself.\n\nThe verse then offers the accusers a test they can run themselves: \"If you ˹polytheists˺ do not know ˹this already˺, then ask those who have knowledge ˹of the Scriptures˺.\" The people of the earlier Scriptures, the Jews and Christians of Arabia, knew from their own books that every prophet had been a human being, eating and walking and dying like other men. The accusers are invited to consult them, because the matter is not a secret of the Quran. The same verse appears almost word for word elsewhere in the Quran, and elsewhere it adds the fuller statement: \"We only sent before you ˹O Prophet˺ men inspired by Us from among the people of each society.\" The verse is an appeal to the oldest testimony available, and it leaves the accusation with nowhere to stand. The objection to a human messenger was never about evidence. It was about the wish for a god who could be seen, and the verse gently turns the wish back toward the facts.",
  "8": "\"We did not give those messengers ˹supernatural˺ bodies that did not need food, nor were they immortal\" completes the answer of the previous verse by removing the last two illusions about prophecy. The messengers were human, and their humanity was visible in the most ordinary ways: they ate, and they died. The verse says their bodies were not made to live without food, and they were not made immortal. Hunger and death, the two things every human being shares, belonged to them as much as to anyone. The Quran makes the same point elsewhere with the same everyday details: \"We never sent any messenger before you ˹O Prophet˺, who did not eat food and go about in market-places.\" The messenger who needs bread and walks through the market is not a failed angel. He is the design, because a message meant for human beings must arrive in human form.\n\nThe verse also carries a quiet rebuke to the demand behind the objection. The accusers wanted a messenger above hunger and beyond death, and the verse asks, in effect, what such a being could have taught them. A creature that does not eat cannot teach the hungry how to be grateful for food. A being that cannot die cannot teach the dying how to prepare for what comes after. The whole point of the human messenger is that his life is the same life as his audience, tested by the same needs and ended by the same death, and still it was lived in obedience. The verse removes the objection, and in removing it, shows why the objection was never the real issue. The verse also contains a hidden comfort for the Prophet's followers in every age. The messenger they follow ate and died like them, and that means the path he walked is a path a human being can actually walk. His example is not the example of a creature from another world; it is the example of a man, and that is precisely why it can be followed.",
  "9": "\"Then We fulfilled Our promise to them\" is the verse of God's faithfulness, and it comes after the description of the messengers as weak, hungry, mortal men. The same messengers who looked so ordinary to their mockers had a promise from God, and the verse says the promise was kept. The pattern is stated in full: \"saving them along with whoever We willed and destroying the transgressors.\" Three groups are named in one sentence. The messengers were saved, the believers God willed to save were saved with them, and the transgressors were destroyed. The word \"transgressors\" is the same word this chapter has used for the wrongdoers, those who crossed the limits, and the verse draws the final line of every prophetic story: deliverance on one side, destruction on the other, and the promise fulfilled exactly.\n\nThe same assurance is given elsewhere in the Quran in the clearest terms: \"We certainly help Our messengers and the believers, ˹both˺ in this worldly life and on the Day the witnesses will stand forth ˹for testimony˺.\" The verse matters for the Prophet and his followers as much as for the readers of the old stories. The messengers looked like ordinary men, and their enemies looked like the owners of the earth, but the promise did not depend on appearances. It depended on God, and it was fulfilled. The verse is the summary of every story this chapter is about to tell, and it teaches the reader to read each story with the ending already in view: the messenger is saved, the transgressor is not, and the promise never fails. The verse also explains why the stories of the prophets are told the way they are told. Each one ends with the same two movements, the saving and the destroying, because the endings are not separate events but one law, and the listener is meant to recognize the law by the time the chapter's stories are finished.",
  "10": "\"We have surely revealed to you a Book, in which there is glory for you\" turns from the stories of the past to the Book in the listener's hands, and the word translated \"glory\" deserves attention. The Arabic is 'dhikr,' which means remembrance and mention, and the commentators explain it in both directions at once. The Book is the remembrance of God, the reminder that keeps Him present in the heart, and the Book is also the remembrance of its people, the honor and renown by which they are mentioned among the nations. A people with no book leaves no lasting name; this Book made its community remembered, and the verse says so plainly: in this Book is your remembrance, your honor, your place in history. The Arabs to whom it was first addressed had no empire and no library, and the verse was telling them that the thing in their hands was worth more than both.\n\nThen the verse closes with its challenge: \"Will you not then understand?\" The word behind \"understand\" is 'aql,' the intellect, and the question is an appeal to reason. The Book's value is not a secret to be taken on faith; it is something a mind can see. The verse is one of the chapter's many appeals to the intellect, and it is aimed at the very thing the earlier verses diagnosed as missing: the people heard and mocked, but they did not think. The question hangs in the air as an invitation. The Book is here, its value is visible, and the only thing standing between the people and their own honor is the failure to understand what they have been given. The question \"Will you not then understand?\" is also the chapter's recurring appeal, and it deserves to be taken literally. The verse is not asking for emotion or for blind acceptance. It is asking the listener to use the mind, because the Book's claim to be a remembrance and an honor is a claim that reason can examine and history can test.",
  "11": "\"˹Imagine˺ how many societies of wrongdoers We have destroyed, raising up other people after them!\" continues the theme of the destroyed nations, and the word \"Imagine\" is the verse's way of making the listener do the work. The Arabic is 'qasamna,' how many societies We crushed and broke, and the verse does not name them, because the listeners already knew them from their own landscape and their own traditions. The key addition in this verse is the second half: \"raising up other people after them\". The destroyed societies were not the end of history. Other peoples were raised in their place, and the world moved on without them, the way a field is replanted after a harvest. This is the deepest cut in the warning. To be destroyed is terrible, but to be replaced, to have one's whole people written out of the story while new nations occupy their homes, is the complete erasure of everything a society thought it was.\n\nThe verse is also a comfort folded inside the warning. The same law that removes the wrongdoers also raises the replacements, and the believers who were oppressed by the wrongdoers saw the turnover with their own eyes. What looks like the permanent order of the world is actually a rotation. The tyrant's society feels eternal to itself, and the verse says it is one entry in a long list of broken and replaced peoples. The word \"wrongdoers\" is the key: the destruction is tied to the wrongdoing, not to chance. God replaces the unjust with others, and the verse invites the listener to read the entire history of the region as the record of that replacement. The same rotation has run through every age since. Empires that seemed permanent to their own citizens have been broken and replaced, and the people who replaced them walked through the ruins without remembering the names. The verse is the Quran's way of keeping the names and the lesson attached to them.",
  "12": "\"When the wrongdoers sensed ˹the arrival of˺ Our torment, they started to run away from their cities\" describes the first response of the condemned nations, and the detail is almost comic in its futility. The word \"sensed\" is 'ahassu,' to feel something arriving, and what they felt was the punishment of God already upon them. Their reaction was not repentance; it was flight. They ran from their cities, the fortresses and walls that had made them feel untouchable, as if the punishment were a flood that could be outrun or an army that could be escaped. The verse lets the absurdity stand without comment. The punishment of God is not a pursuer that tires. It is a decree that arrives, and the fastest legs in the city cannot carry anyone out of its reach.\n\nThe commentators note the psychology the verse captures. The wrongdoers had lived behind their walls, mocking the warnings, confident that their cities made them safe. When the punishment arrived, the first thing they discovered was that the walls meant nothing, and the second was that their own legs were their only plan. There is also a deeper meaning in the flight itself. The city was where their wrongdoing lived, their wealth, their festivals, their pride, and leaving it behind was an admission that none of it could help them. The verse is the first movement of the scene the next verses will complete, and it shows the wrongdoer's oldest illusion, the illusion of escape, dying in real time. The verse also prepares the ground for the chapter's larger argument about the Day of Judgment. If the punishments of this world cannot be outrun, then the punishment of the Hereafter, which is greater, cannot be outrun either. The fleeing of the wrongdoers is the picture of every attempt to escape the account, and the picture ends the same way every time: the account arrives.",
  "13": "\"Do not run away! Return to your luxuries and your homes, so you may be questioned ˹about your fate˺\" is the command the fleeing people receive, and the commentators explain that it is spoken in mockery, the way the angels or the divine address derides them as they run. The irony is exact. They are fleeing the punishment, and they are told to run back into the life they were so proud of: their luxuries, the word 'taraf' for the fine things and comfortable living, and their homes, the places where they felt safe. Return to what you trusted, the voice says, and let it be questioned along with you. Every luxury they accumulated and every wall they built is now an item in the case against them, because the comforts were the very things that made them heedless.\n\nThe verse teaches something about the relationship between comfort and heedlessness that runs through the whole Quran. The wrongdoers were not destroyed for being comfortable. They were destroyed for letting the comfort become their god, for measuring their safety by their possessions instead of their deeds. The command to return is the punishment's way of showing them what they actually worshipped. Run back to it, the voice says, and see if it answers when you are questioned. The scene is the Quran's sharpest picture of the moment when a life's investments are revealed as nothing, and the verse leaves the reader to imagine the people turning back toward their doomed houses, with the punishment already in the sky above them. The verse is also a study in the meaning of irony. The voice tells them the exact opposite of what they expect to hear, and the mockery is earned, because they had spent their lives mocking the warning that told them this day would come. The Quran rarely speaks in mockery, and when it does, it is returning to the mockers the tone they themselves established.",
  "14": "\"Woe to us! We have surely been wrongdoers\" is the confession of the condemned, and it arrives at exactly the wrong time. The words are the right words. They admit the wrongdoing, they grieve over themselves, and the word \"woe\" is the Quran's cry of total loss. But the verse places the confession after the punishment has already arrived, when the running has failed and the command to return has exposed them, and the chapter will state in the next verse what happened to their crying. The confession is real and useless at once, and the Quran records it to teach the most important lesson about repentance: it has an expiry date. The admission that comes when the punishment is visible is not repentance; it is the scream of the caught, and it does not turn the decree back.\n\nThe verse also shows what the wrongdoers knew all along. No one in these scenes pleads ignorance. The moment the punishment arrives, they say \"We have surely been wrongdoers\", and the speed of the confession proves that the knowledge was always there, buried under the mockery and the comfort. The Quran elsewhere records the same pattern in the drowning Pharaoh, whose faith came only when the sea closed over him. The verse is a warning written in the voice of the losers themselves: the words you plan to say at the end are already known, and saying them at the end is not the same as saying them in time. The confession of this verse is what remains when the chance to confess is gone. The verse is also the Quran's way of teaching the difference between regret and repentance. Regret is what these people felt, the sorrow of being caught. Repentance is the turning that happens while there is still time to turn, and the whole chapter is an appeal to make the second choice while it exists.",
  "15": "\"They kept repeating their cry until We mowed them down, ˹leaving them˺ lifeless\" closes the scene with one of the Quran's most haunting images, and the word behind \"mowed them down\" is 'hasidin,' the word for reaping a field at harvest. The condemned people, still repeating their cry of woe, are cut down the way a crop is cut, and the word \"lifeless\" completes the picture: what is left is not a people but a field of still bodies. The image is deliberate. All through their lives they had thought of themselves as the owners of the land, and the verse reveals what they actually were: a crop whose harvest time had come. The reaping was not random, and the repetition of the cry until the end shows that nothing changed in their final moments. The confession did not stop the scythe.\n\nThe verse also answers a question the scene might raise. Why does the Quran tell the story of a people crying out and being cut down without mercy? Because the mercy had already been offered, and refused, for as long as the warning lasted. The prophets came, the signs came, and the time for the cry to work was then. The verse is the Quran's way of showing that the Day of Judgment will not be a negotiation. The harvest image runs through the whole chapter's argument: life is the growing season, the warning is the notice, and the reaping is certain. The verse leaves the reader inside the field with the question the chapter keeps asking: which side of the harvest will you be standing on when it comes? The verse is also a warning about the speed of the end. The crop image has no slow dying in it; the scythe moves, and the field is down. The people who think they will have time to repent at the last moment are assuming a slow harvest, and the verse removes the assumption. When the reaping begins, it finishes.",
  "16": "\"We did not create the heavens and the earth and everything in between for sport\" states the purpose of creation in the negative, and the negative is stronger than a positive statement would be. The word \"sport\" is 'la'ib,' play, and the verse denies that the universe is a game. The heavens with their stars, the earth with its life, and everything between them were not made as an amusement, and the verse wants the listener to feel the absurdity of the alternative. A universe of such order, in which night follows day and the seed knows how to grow, carrying a label that says it was all a game, would be the strangest thing imaginable, and the verse says it plainly: it was not for sport. The same statement appears elsewhere with its reason attached: \"We have not created the heavens and earth and everything in between without purpose—as the disbelievers think.\"\n\nThe verse is aimed at a specific way of living. The people who treat the world as a playground, who take its pleasures and ignore its warnings, are living as if creation had no purpose, and the verse tells them that their lifestyle contradicts the nature of what God made. The Quran asks the question directly elsewhere: \"Did you then think that We had created you without purpose, and that you would never be returned to Us?\" Purpose is written into the structure of everything, and the human being is the one creature who can choose to live against it. The verse is the first of several in this passage that will build the case: creation is serious, its Creator is serious, and the account that follows is serious. Play is what people do inside the world; it is not what the world is. The verse also begins a sequence that the next verses will complete: if creation is not a game, then its Creator is not a player, and if its Creator is not a player, then the account He promised is real. The three verses work as one argument, and each one hands its conclusion to the next.",
  "17": "\"Had We intended to take ˹some˺ amusement, We could have found it in Our presence, if that had been Our Will\" presses the argument of the previous verse to its logical end, and the logic is devastating. Play and amusement are the resorts of those who lack something: the bored play to fill time, the weary play to rest, and the needy play to forget. God lacks nothing, needs no rest, and has no time to fill. If amusement were something God could be imagined to want, He would not need to create a universe to obtain it; He could have found it with Himself. The verse is the Quran's way of clearing God of every human motive that people project onto Him. The Creator is not a being who made the world because He was bored or lonely or in need of entertainment, and the very suggestion is shown to be incoherent.\n\nThe verse also quietly dismantles the worldview of the heedless. The people who live for amusement assume that amusement is the highest thing there is, so they imagine that even a god, if there is one, must be after amusement. The verse turns the assumption around: the One who is self-sufficient has no use for play, and the universe He made is not a toy but a serious work with a serious end. The commentators note the gentleness of the form: the verse says \"if that had been Our Will\", leaving the matter to God's will, while making clear that such a will is unthinkable for the One who is above all need. The next verse will show what God actually does instead of playing: He hurls the truth at falsehood, and the hurling is the opposite of a game. The verse also clears the ground for taking the Hereafter seriously. If the universe is not God's amusement, then the human being is not an amusement either, and the account each person owes is not a game's score but a real debt. The next verse states the law that governs this serious creation.",
  "18": "\"In fact, We hurl the truth against falsehood, leaving it crushed, and it quickly vanishes\" replaces the idea of play with the real law of the universe, and the imagery is physical and final. The verb \"hurl\" is 'qadhf,' to throw something with force, and the word behind \"crushed\" is 'damgh,' a word that means to strike something on the head and shatter it. The picture is of a blow aimed at the skull of falsehood, and the result is described twice for emphasis: it is crushed, and it vanishes. Falsehood is not defeated gradually, the verse says. It is struck, and it is gone, the way a bubble is gone the moment it is touched. The truth is not one contestant in a long match against error; it is the thing thrown, and error is the thing broken.\n\nThe verse is also an answer to everyone who has ever watched falsehood prosper and wondered whether the fight is even. The world often looks like falsehood is winning, and the verse says the look is temporary. Falsehood has no foundation, and a single strike of the truth empties it. Then the verse turns to its audience with the warning that follows from the law: \"And woe be to you for what you claim!\" The word \"woe\" is 'wayl,' the cry of ruin, and the claims named are the lies about God that the chapter has been recording, the invented gods and the mockery. Those who build their lives on falsehood are standing where the blow will land. The verse is one of the Quran's most compact statements of confidence: the truth is not merely defended by God; it is His instrument, and falsehood's days are always numbered. The verse also explains why the believer can remain calm while falsehood appears to prosper. The prosperity is not strength; it is the last moment before the strike. Falsehood's size is never the measure of its future, because the truth is the one thing in the universe that cannot be broken, and everything else is waiting to be struck by it.",
  "19": "\"To Him belong all those in the heavens and the earth\" opens a new argument, and it is the argument from ownership. Everything that exists belongs to God, the verse says, and the statement is total: all who are in the heavens and all who are on the earth. There is no creature, visible or invisible, that falls outside this ownership, and the verse places the claim at the head of its argument because everything that follows rests on it. The pagans of Mecca had taken gods for themselves, and the first thing the verse does is remind them that the beings they worship are themselves owned. A god who is owned cannot be a god, and the verse begins by dissolving the whole pantheon with one sentence.\n\nThen the verse points to the beings the pagans respected most: \"And those nearest to Him are not too proud to worship Him, nor do they tire.\" The \"nearest\" are the angels, the highest creatures in existence, and the verse reports their state: they worship, they are not too proud, and they never tire. The contrast is aimed at the pagans, who were too proud to worship the true God while bowing to stones, and the verse lets the comparison do its work. The Quran states the same scene elsewhere: \"Surely those ˹angels˺ nearest to your Lord are not too proud to worship Him. They glorify Him. And to Him they prostrate.\" If the highest beings in creation worship God without pride or weariness, what excuse does a human being have for pride? The verse is the chapter's way of showing the true order of existence: everything is owned, everything worships, and the only creature that imagines itself exempt is the one with the most to lose. The verse is also a correction of a common confusion about worship. The angels' worship is not a weakness in them and it is not a favor they do for God; it is the natural state of the highest creatures before their Lord. The believer who understands this verse stops measuring worship as a burden and begins to see it as the nearest thing to the angels' station that a human being can reach.",
  "20": "\"They glorify ˹Him˺ day and night, never wavering\" completes the picture of the angels that the previous verse began, and the verse is a single line that contains an entire standard. The angels glorify God day and night, meaning continuously, without a pause, and the phrase \"never wavering\" translates 'la yafturun,' which means they do not slacken or grow tired. The word is chosen to answer the previous verse's mention of weariness: the angels do not tire, and their worship does not fade. There is no angel who worships only on certain days, no angel who grows bored of glorification, and no angel who treats worship as one activity among others. Their whole existence is glorification, and the verse says it never weakens.\n\nThe verse is placed here for a purpose that goes beyond describing the angels. The chapter is arguing with people who found worship burdensome, who mocked the call to prayer and preferred their festivals and games, and the verse holds up the angels as the mirror. The beings nearest to God, the ones the pagans half-respected, worship without stopping, while the human beings who owe everything to God find an hour of worship too much. The commentators note that the verse is an invitation to measure human worship against the standard it describes. The believer is not expected to worship like an angel, but the verse shows the direction worship points: toward constancy, toward joy, toward a life in which glorifying God is not a break from living but the form of it. The next verses will return to the pagans and their invented gods, and the image of the never-tiring angels will stand behind the argument. There is also a practical reading of the verse that the early scholars drew from it: worship is meant to be continuous in its spirit even when its forms are spread through the day. The believer's prayer times are the points of the day, but the glorification behind them is meant to be the texture of the whole life.",
  "21": "\"Or have they taken gods from the earth, who can raise the dead?\" is the chapter's sarcastic question about the idols, and the word \"or\" connects it to everything before. The pagans have been shown that creation is purposeful, that God is above play, and that even the angels worship Him, and now the verse turns to the gods they actually chose. They took gods \"from the earth,\" and the phrase is a put-down by location: their gods are earthly, made of the same stone and wood as the ground they stand on. Then comes the test that exposes the whole business: \"who can raise the dead?\" The question is the verse's entire argument, because resurrection is the one thing a god must be able to do. A god who cannot give life cannot be a god, and the idols, which cannot even move themselves, are dismissed by the question without needing another word.\n\nThe question also connects to the chapter's larger theme. The Quran keeps returning to the resurrection because it is the test case of divine power, and the pagans who denied the resurrection were, in effect, worshipping gods who shared their own limitation. The verse forces the choice into the open: either your gods can raise the dead, in which case show it, or they cannot, in which case what are they? The commentators note the verse's tone: it is not asking for information but exposing an absurdity, the way one would ask a man bowing to a statue whether the statue has ever done anything. The next verse will expand the argument into its full form, the argument of the two gods, and this question is its sharpest edge. A god of the earth is still just earth, and earth does not raise the dead. The question is also a bridge to the next verse. Having shown that their gods cannot raise the dead, the Quran will now ask what the universe would look like if such gods were real, and the answer will complete the destruction of the claim. The question here is the first blow, and the next verse is the final one.",
  "22": "\"Had there been other gods besides Allah in the heavens or the earth, both ˹realms˺ would have surely been corrupted\" is the argument that classical commentators call the proof of mutual exclusion, and it is one of the most famous arguments in the Quran. The reasoning is simple and complete. If there were two gods, or more, each with real power, what would stop them from conflicting? Each would want the universe run its way, and the result of two supreme wills would be collision and chaos, the same way two kings cannot rule one kingdom, and the same way two captains cannot steer one ship. The fact that the universe runs in order, with its laws steady and its systems dependable, is the visible proof that one will governs it. The verse compresses the whole proof into a single sentence: two gods would mean corruption, and there is no corruption in the design, so there is one God.\n\nThe same argument appears elsewhere with the details filled in: \"Otherwise, each god would have taken away what he created, and they would have tried to dominate one another.\" Each god would seize his own creation and each would fight for supremacy, and the result would be exactly the disorder the universe does not show. The verse then closes with the declaration the argument supports: \"So Glorified is Allah, Lord of the Throne, far above what they claim.\" The Throne is the Quran's image of God's supreme authority over all creation, and the glorification declares Him above every partner the pagans invented. The verse is a complete proof in three movements: the observation, the inference, and the praise, and it has been taught to believers for centuries as the clearest reasoning the Quran offers for the oneness of God. The argument is also a comfort to the believer who studies the world's order, because every law of nature that holds steady is a witness to the verse. The sunrise that does not fail and the seasons that do not collide are the signature of one will, and the verse invites the mind to read the signature in everything.",
  "23": "\"He cannot be questioned about what He does, but they will ˹all˺ be questioned\" states the boundary between the Judge and the judged, and it is a verse about authority, not about silence. God cannot be questioned, the verse says, because questioning is what happens to someone who must answer to someone else. God answers to no one. His actions come from perfect knowledge and wisdom, and there is no higher court before which His decisions could be reviewed. The creatures, by contrast, will all be questioned, every one of them, about what they did. The verse places the two sides of existence in their proper positions: the One who acts without being answerable, and the many who act and must answer. The verse is the Quran's way of settling the matter that human pride keeps reopening, the wish to put God on trial for the way the world runs.\n\nThe verse also teaches the right posture for the believer before the things God does that a human mind cannot fully grasp. It is not a ban on asking about wisdom; the Quran itself explains the wisdom behind much of what God does. It is a boundary against the kind of questioning that presumes to judge, the demand that God's decisions be justified before the bench of human opinion. The commentators explain the verse's place in the chapter: the pagans had been arguing with God's choices, questioning why the messenger was human and why the punishment was delayed, and the verse answers the whole attitude at its root. The created do not sit in judgement over the Creator. The direction of questioning runs one way, and the verse says it in the plainest words: He is not questioned, and they are. The verse is also a shield against despair and against arrogance at the same time. It frees the believer from the burden of judging God's decree, which no human mind can carry, and it frees no one from the burden of answering for their own deeds, which every human being will carry. The boundary the verse draws is the boundary of peace.",
  "24": "\"Or have they taken other gods besides Him? Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Show ˹me˺ your proof\"\" turns from argument to evidence, and the demand is the fairest demand that can be made. The pagans had taken gods besides God, and the Prophet is told to ask them for their proof. The word \"proof\" is 'burhan,' the clearest kind of evidence, and the verse is inviting the pagans to produce anything that supports their claim: a scripture, a sign, a reason. The Quran is confident enough to ask, because the answer is known in advance: there is no proof for a false god, and the demand exposes the emptiness without needing to argue further. Then the verse presents the other side of the ledger: \"Here is ˹the Quran,˺ the Reminder for those with me; along with ˹earlier Scriptures,˺ the Reminder for those before me.\" The believers have their proof: this Book, and the earlier Scriptures behind it, a chain of divine reminders reaching back through all the prophets. Proof on one side, nothing on the other.\n\nThe verse then diagnoses why the pagans do not accept what is in front of them: \"But most of them do not know the truth, so they turn away.\" The word \"know\" is the key. They do not lack the evidence; they lack the knowledge, because the heart that will not look cannot know, and the knowing they avoid is what makes them turn away. The verse is a complete courtroom scene: the claim is examined, the proof is demanded, the evidence is compared, and the verdict explains the rejection. It is also the chapter's answer to the earlier demand for signs. The sign they asked for was a spectacle; the proof they were given is a Book, and the Book is the stronger evidence, because a spectacle passes and a Book remains, demanding to be read.",
  "25": "\"We never sent a messenger before you ˹O Prophet˺ without revealing to him\" introduces the chapter's most important statement, and the statement is about the unity of the message itself. The verse says that every messenger God ever sent received the same revelation, and then it quotes the revelation: \"There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Me, so worship Me ˹alone˺.\" This is the creed of every prophet from the first to the last, the single sentence that runs through all of revelation. Noah said it, Abraham said it, Moses said it, Jesus said it, and Muhammad said it, and the chapter that is named after the prophets places the sentence at the center of everything. The messengers differed in law and language, but the foundation never changed, and the verse makes the point absolute: never a messenger, without this revelation.\n\nThe same truth is stated elsewhere in the chapter's voice: \"We surely sent a messenger to every community, saying, \"Worship Allah and shun false gods.\"\" The verse is also the answer to every claim that the prophets preached different gods or rival religions. They preached one God and one worship, and the differences that later grew up around them were additions, not origins. The verse lands with particular force in this chapter, because the pagans of Mecca had filled the space between the prophets with their own gods, and the Quran is telling them that every prophet they half-believed in had denounced exactly what they were doing. The message of all the prophets is quoted in one line, and the line is the simplest thing in the Quran: no god but Me, so worship Me. Everything else in the chapter's stories is commentary on that sentence. The verse also gives the reader the simplest test of any claim to prophecy or guidance: does it call to the worship of God alone? Every genuine messenger came with that call, and anything that points away from it is not an extension of the prophets' message but a departure from it. The chapter named after the prophets closes this passage with the test, and the test has never needed updating.",
  "26": "And they say, \"The Most Compassionate has offspring!\" is the claim the verse quotes before it demolishes it, and the claim belongs to the pagans of Arabia. They said the angels were God's daughters, and they worshipped them partly for that reason. The verse answers with the Quran's shortest and most complete reply: \"Glory be to Him!\" The word 'subhanahu' declares God far above the very possibility of what they said. The One who owns everything, who needs nothing, and who has no equal cannot have offspring, because offspring is what a being produces when it needs a successor, a helper, or a continuation of itself, and God is above all three needs. The same claim is answered elsewhere in almost the same words: \"They say, \"Allah has offspring.\" Glory be to Him! In fact, to Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth—all are subject to His Will.\"\n\nThe verse then corrects the claim at its root: \"In fact, those ˹angels˺ are only ˹His˺ honoured servants\". The beings the pagans called God's daughters are servants, owned, obedient, and honoured by their service rather than by any share in divinity. The verse shifts the whole picture in one phrase. The pagans thought the angels' closeness to God made them divine; the verse says their closeness makes them servants, and the honour of a servant is in serving. The next verses will describe what these honoured servants actually do, and the description will show how far the truth of the angels is from the myth the pagans had built around them. The verse is the chapter's first blow against the idea of divine offspring, and it lands on the word \"servants\". The next two verses will describe what these servants do, and each detail of their obedience will be another blow against the claim, until the idea of divine offspring is left with nothing to stand on.",
  "27": "\"who do not speak until He has spoken, ˹only˺ acting at His command\" continues the description of the angels, and it defines their entire existence in one line. They do not speak before God speaks, and they do not act except at His command. Two freedoms that human beings constantly claim for themselves, the freedom of the tongue and the freedom of the hand, are not claimed by the angels, and the verse presents their state as the model of perfect obedience. The pagans had imagined the angels as independent beings with divine status, and the verse corrects the imagination with the simplest of facts: the beings they worshipped are themselves waiting for permission.\n\nThe verse also draws the line between servanthood and divinity with total clarity. A god is the one who commands; a servant is the one who waits for the command. The angels wait, so they are servants, and the claim that they are God's offspring collapses under its own weight. The commentators note the precision of the verse's two halves: speech comes first, because speech is the beginning of action, and if the angels do not speak until God has spoken, then nothing they do can be their own initiative. The verse is one sentence, and it contains the whole correction: the honoured ones of the heavens do not speak first and do not act first, and the One they wait for is the only One who ever speaks first. The next verse will add the third dimension of their servanthood, their knowledge and their intercession. There is also a lesson for the reader folded into the description. The angels are not praised for occasional obedience; their entire existence is obedience, and the verse holds that existence up as the pattern of what a creature is meant to be. The human being is not asked to be an angel, but the direction of the heart is the same: to wait for God's word before speaking, and to seek His command before acting.",
  "28": "\"He ˹fully˺ knows what is ahead of them and what is behind them\" states the third dimension of the angels' servanthood, and it concerns knowledge. God knows everything that lies before the angels and everything that lies behind them, their future and their past, and the verse is saying that even the highest creatures exist inside God's knowledge. They do not know their own tomorrows; the One they serve knows everything about them. The phrase \"ahead\" and \"behind\" covers all of time as human minds experience it, and the verse places the angels, for all their honour, inside that knowledge rather than above it.\n\nThen the verse turns to the two things the pagans expected from the angels: \"They do not intercede except for whom He approves, and they tremble in awe of Him.\" The pagans worshipped the angels hoping for their intercession, and the verse says the intercession of even these honoured beings is not theirs to give. It is granted, case by case, only for those God approves, and the permission comes from Him alone. The same principle is stated in the greatest verse of the Quran: \"Who could possibly intercede with Him without His permission?\" And then the last stroke: \"they tremble in awe of Him.\" The beings the pagans bowed to are themselves trembling before God, and the word for awe is the word of fear mixed with reverence. The verse completes the picture of the true angels: they do not speak first, they do not act first, they do not know beyond what God knows, they do not intercede on their own, and they tremble. Every one of these facts is a nail in the coffin of the claim that they are gods or God's children. The verse also corrects the instinct that led the pagans to worship the angels in the first place. They saw beings of great rank and assumed that rank meant independence. The verse shows that the angels' rank is exactly their dependence, and that true greatness in creation is measured by nearness to God through obedience, not by distance from Him through claims.",
  "29": "Whoever of them were to say, \"I am a god besides Him,\" they would be rewarded with Hell by Us announces the law that governs even the angels, and it is the verse that closes the whole discussion. If any angel, with all their honour and nearness, were to claim divinity, the claim would earn them Hell. The verse is conditional in form, \"Whoever of them were to say\", because no angel would ever say it, but the condition is stated to show that even the highest beings are not exempt from the law. Nearness to God is not immunity from God's justice. The angels are honoured because they serve, and the moment any creature stops serving and starts claiming, the honour ends and the reward changes to Hell. The verse then states the general principle: \"This is how We reward the wrongdoers.\" The claim to divinity is the ultimate wrongdoing, because it takes the position that belongs to God alone, and the reward for it is fixed for angels and humans alike.\n\nThe verse also answers a question the pagans never asked but should have: what would God do if one of the beings they worshipped actually claimed to be a god? The answer is given in advance, and it strips the whole pantheon of any fear or mystery. No being in the heavens or the earth can claim divinity and survive the claim. The verse is the chapter's way of saying that the boundary between Creator and creature is guarded by an absolute law, and the law applies upward as much as downward. If the nearest of the near would be thrown into Hell for the claim, then what is the position of the idol, the star, or the human being who makes the same claim? The question needs no answer, and the verse moves on to the signs of creation that make the whole argument unnecessary.",
  "30": "\"Do the disbelievers not realize that the heavens and earth were ˹once˺ one mass then We split them apart?\" opens the great passage of the signs, and it begins with the most fundamental question the Quran can ask: do they not see? The verse points to the origin of the universe, the single mass that was split, and it presents the fact not as a scientific discovery but as a sign that has been visible to the mind all along. The heavens and the earth, with everything in them, began as one joined entity, and God split them apart, and the verse asks whether the people who deny the resurrection have ever considered what that beginning means. The One who split the mass and made the heavens and the earth can certainly split the grave and make the dead rise. The beginning of creation is the proof of its renewal, and the verse states the beginning in one line.\n\nThe second sign follows: \"And We created from water every living thing.\" Every living creature, the verse says, is made from water, and the claim is stated as a fact about all of life. The commentators note that the verse points to the dependence of every living thing on water, the substance without which nothing lives, and the same statement appears elsewhere with its detail expanded: \"And Allah has created from water every living creature. Some of them crawl on their bellies, some walk on two legs, and some walk on four.\" Then the verse closes with the question that the whole passage is driving toward: \"Will they not then believe?\" The signs are in front of them, the origin of the universe and the substance of life, and the question asks what more the heart needs. The verse is the Quran's method in miniature: point to the creation, and let the creation testify to the Creator.",
  "31": "\"And We have placed firm mountains upon the earth so it does not shake with them\" moves from the heavens to the ground underfoot, and the verse states the purpose of the mountains in the plainest words. The mountains are not scenery. They are weights and anchors, placed into the earth so that it holds steady for the life on it. The word \"firm\" renders 'rawasi,' the word for the anchors of a ship, and the image is exact: the mountains hold the earth the way anchors hold a vessel, so that the ground does not shake under its inhabitants. The verse is part of the Quran's repeated description of the earth as a prepared home, and every detail of the preparation is named as a kindness: the mountains that steady it, the pathways that open it, and the provisions that come from it.\n\nThe second half of the verse adds the pathways: \"and made in it broad pathways so they may find their way.\" The word for \"pathways\" is 'fijajan,' wide passes and routes, and the purpose is stated: so that people can find their way. The earth is not only steady; it is navigable, crossed by routes that connect its people to one another and to their livelihoods. The same verse appears elsewhere with the rivers added: \"He has placed into the earth firm mountains, so it does not shake with you, as well as rivers, and pathways so you may find your way.\" The verse is a list of favours dressed as geography, and each item is an argument. The earth does not steady itself, and the paths do not open themselves, and the verse leaves the conclusion to the reader: the One who anchored the ground and opened the routes is the One who deserves the worship that the idols are receiving.",
  "32": "\"And We have made the sky a well-protected canopy, still they turn away from its signs\" turns the gaze upward, and the description of the sky begins with a word of protection. The sky is called a canopy, 'saqfan,' a roof, and the word \"well-protected\" renders 'mahfuzan,' guarded and preserved. The sky is both a shelter and a guard, and the commentators explain the two sides of the protection: it shelters the earth from what would harm it, and it is itself guarded, kept from collapsing and kept from being breached by what should not pass. The Quran elsewhere describes the guarding of the sky against the devils who try to listen: \"And We protected it from every accursed devil\". The verse is part of a larger picture of the sky as a deliberate structure, a roof built and maintained, and every roof implies a builder.\n\nThen the verse states the human response to the structure: \"still they turn away from its signs.\" The word \"signs\" is 'ayat,' the same word the Quran uses for its own verses, and the verse is deliberately linking the two: the signs in the sky and the signs in the Book both point to the same truth, and the people turn away from both. The commentators note the tone of the verse: the sky with all its order is standing over their heads every day and night, and they do not look at it with the eyes that see. The verse is the shortest possible summary of the human condition the chapter keeps describing: the signs are everywhere, and the turning away is everywhere, and between the two stands the choice the chapter is trying to turn around. The verse also pairs the two kinds of signs on purpose. The sky is a sign that needs no messenger to deliver it, and the Book is a sign that needs no telescope to read it. Both are available to every person, and the verse asks why the people who live under the one and hear the other have turned away from both.",
  "33": "\"And He is the One Who created the day and the night, the sun and the moon—each travelling in an orbit\" continues the list of the signs, and this verse points to the two great clocks of the world. Day and night are created things, not properties of the earth that made themselves, and the sun and the moon are created things too, and the verse pairs them deliberately because the pagans of Arabia had made the sun and the moon objects of worship. The verse's answer to sun-worship and moon-worship is not a lecture but a description: they are created, and they travel in orbits. The word \"orbit\" is 'falak,' and the verse says each one moves in its own course, held in a path it did not choose. A god does not travel in an orbit; a servant does, and the sun and the moon are the most obedient servants in the sky.\n\nThe same statement appears elsewhere with the precision that has made the verse famous: \"It is not for the sun to catch up with the moon, nor does the night outrun the day. Each is travelling in an orbit of their own.\" The sun cannot leave its course to collide with the moon, and the night cannot overtake the day, because both are running on orders. The verse is the Quran's way of turning astronomy into worship: the more precisely the heavens move, the more clearly they testify to the One who set them moving. The day and the night, the sun and the moon, each in its orbit: the verse lists them without comment, because the order itself is the comment, and the order is the signature of the One who created it. The verse also touches the reader's own daily experience. Every sunset and sunrise is the orbit continuing, and every month of the moon is the course completing, and the person who notices the regularity has seen what the verse is pointing at. The sky is the most-read page of creation, and the verse is teaching the reader to read it as worship.",
  "34": "\"We have not granted immortality to any human before you ˹O Prophet˺: so if you die, will they live forever?\" is the verse that faces death head-on, and it is spoken in the Prophet's own situation. The disbelievers of Mecca used to wait for Muhammad's death, telling one another that his message would die with him, and the verse answers the waiting with a fact and a question. The fact: no human being before him was given immortality, so death is not a scandal in his case; it is the common road of every prophet and every person. The question: if he dies, will they live forever? The question expects no answer, because the answer is obvious, and the obviousness is the point. The people who mock the Prophet's mortality are themselves mortal, and the verse turns their waiting into a mirror. They wait for his death, and death is waiting for them.\n\nThe same argument appears elsewhere in the Quran in its fullest form: \"Muḥammad is no more than a messenger; other messengers have gone before him. If he were to die or to be killed, would you regress into disbelief?\" The verse is also a comfort to the Prophet and his followers. The message does not depend on one life, because the message belongs to the One who never dies. The mockers thought they were pointing at a weakness, and the verse shows that they were pointing at the nature of every human being, themselves included. Death is not the refutation of a messenger; it is the proof that the messenger was human, and the humanity of the messenger was never the flaw the mockers thought it was. The next verse will state the law of death for everyone, and the statement leaves no exceptions. The verse also frees the believer from an unspoken fear: the fear that the loss of a loved leader is the loss of the cause. Causes that depend on a single life are human causes; the divine message outlives every messenger, because its keeper never dies.",
  "35": "\"Every soul will taste death\" states the law in its shortest possible form, and the word \"every\" does the work. No exception is named, no class is spared, and no achievement postpones it. Every soul, the believer and the denier, the prophet and the tyrant, will taste death, and the word \"taste\" is chosen with care: death is tasted, experienced, known, the way a person tastes something once and remembers it forever. The same sentence appears elsewhere in the Quran, and the repetition marks it as one of the few laws that the Quran states without condition and without exception. Then the verse moves to the second law: \"And We test you ˹O humanity˺ with good and evil as a trial\". Both good and evil are tests, and the verse names both because people usually notice only one. Hardship is a test, and so is ease, and the wealth that makes a person forget God is as much a trial as the poverty that makes a person despair. The Quran states the testing side of hardship elsewhere: \"We will certainly test you with a touch of fear and famine and loss of property, life, and crops.\"\n\nThe verse then closes the circle: \"then to Us you will ˹all˺ be returned.\" Death is not the end of the story, and the tests are not random. Every soul is travelling toward its return, and the return is where the tests are read and the results are known. The verse is the complete frame of human existence in three lines: you will die, you are being tested, and you will return. The chapter's argument about the resurrection rests on this frame, and the verse places it in the middle of the signs of creation, because the return to God is as certain as the death that everyone has already accepted. The mockers who deny the return are asked, in effect, to believe in the first two lines and doubt the third, and the verse leaves the absurdity of that position standing in the open.",
  "36": "\"When the disbelievers see you ˹O Prophet˺, they only make fun of you\" opens the next scene of the chapter, and it moves from the laws of life and death to the street-level mockery of Mecca. The verse describes what happened whenever the disbelievers saw the Prophet: they made fun of him, and their joke is recorded: \"Is this the one who speaks ˹ill˺ of your gods?\" The mockery is aimed at two things at once, the Prophet and his message, and the joke is meant to poison both. By pointing at him as 'this one,' they reduce him to a curiosity, and by saying he speaks ill of their gods, they turn his call to the worship of God alone into an insult to their traditions. The same scene is recorded elsewhere in the Quran: \"When they see you ˹O Prophet˺, they only make fun of you, ˹saying,˺ \"Is this the one that Allah has sent as a messenger?\"\"\n\nThe verse then adds the detail that exposes the mockery for what it is: \"while they disbelieve at the mention of the Most Compassionate.\" They mock the Prophet for speaking ill of their gods, but they themselves turn away in disbelief at the mention of the one true God. The verse is pointing to the double standard at the heart of their position: they defend their gods with mockery, and they reject the true God without a thought. The word \"Most Compassionate\" is chosen deliberately, because the name of God that stresses His mercy is the very name they refuse to hear, and their refusal shows that their problem is not with mockery or respect. It is with God Himself. The verse is the chapter's portrait of mockery as a religion in reverse: what deserves reverence is mocked, and what deserves mockery is reverenced, and the people doing both think they are the clever ones.",
  "37": "\"Humankind is made of haste\" is one of the Quran's most famous diagnoses of human nature, and it comes as the answer to the mockery of the previous verse. The word \"haste\" is 'ajal,' the rush that wants everything now, and the verse says it is built into the human being. The people who mocked the Prophet's warnings were, in their own way, hasty: they wanted the punishment now, they wanted the proof now, and they treated the delay as evidence that the threat was empty. The verse answers by naming the rush itself as the problem. The same diagnosis appears elsewhere: \"And humans ˹swiftly˺ pray for evil as they pray for good. For humankind is ever hasty.\" Human beings ask for things quickly, and what they ask for quickly is often the very thing that would destroy them.\n\nThe second half of the verse answers the demand that comes from the haste: \"I will soon show you My signs, so do not ask Me to hasten them.\" The word \"soon\" is the Quran's way of saying that the signs and the fulfilment are coming on their own schedule, and the schedule is God's, not the mockers'. The verse is both a promise and a rebuke. It promises that the signs will come, and it rebukes the demand that they come now, because the one who asks to hasten the punishment is asking to hasten his own ruin. The commentators note the mercy inside the delay: God does not answer the hasty prayer for punishment, because the delay itself is the chance to repent. The verse teaches the reader to see haste as a disease of the heart, and patience as the medicine the Quran keeps prescribing, because everything that matters arrives at its own time. The verse also teaches the reader to recognize haste in its most dangerous form: the impatience with God's plan. The person who demands the punishment now is asking for the end of his own chance, and the verse asks the hasty to trust the timing of the One who sees the whole road.",
  "38": "They ask ˹the believers˺, \"When will this threat come to pass if what you say is true?\" records the question that the mockers asked, and the question is as old as prophecy itself. Every people who ever heard a warning asked it: when? The word \"threat\" is their word, not the believers', because they heard the promise of judgement as a threat, and they asked for its date as if the date were the weakness of the claim. If the punishment is real, they reasoned, then when is it, and the delay became their proof that it would never come. The verse records the question in their own wording, and the chapter will answer it in the next verses, but the answer will not be a calendar. The answer will be a description of what the day will be like when it comes, because the question \"when\" is the wrong question, and the Quran keeps redirecting it toward the question that matters: what will it be like, and where will you be standing?\n\nThe verse is also a portrait of a certain kind of skepticism. The mockers did not ask about the content of the warning, and they did not ask what they should do to be safe from it. They asked for its timing, because timing is the one thing that lets a person postpone. As long as the date is unknown, the warning can be filed away, and the question 'when' is really a way of saying 'not now.' The chapter answers by refusing to file it away. The next verses will bring the day itself into the present, so close and so sudden that the question \"when\" becomes irrelevant, and the only question left is the one the mockers never asked: what will protect you when it comes?",
  "39": "\"If only the disbelievers knew that a time will come when they will not be able to keep the Fire off their faces or backs, nor will they be helped\" is the answer to the question of the previous verse, and it answers by bringing the day into the present. The phrase \"If only the disbelievers knew\" is the Quran's way of grieving over ignorance that is chosen, and what they do not know is described in physical detail: a time will come when the Fire will be on them, and they will not be able to keep it off their faces or their backs. The two parts of the body are named because they are the most precious and the most exposed: the face, which a person protects before anything else, and the back, which a person cannot see. On that day, there will be no protection for either, and the verse says they will not be helped. The help they counted on, the gods, the wealth, the connections, will all be absent, and the verse states the absence in advance.\n\nThe verse is an answer to the mockers' \"when\" by turning the question around. They asked when the threat would come, and the verse says: if you knew what it is, you would not ask when. You would ask how to escape it. The commentators note the gentleness inside the terror: the verse says \"If only the disbelievers knew\", which means the door of knowing is still open. The warning is being given precisely so that the day, when it comes, does not find them unprepared. The verse is the chapter's way of describing the Fire before the Fire, and the description is an act of mercy, because the one who sees the warning in time can still turn away from what the warning describes. The next verse will describe the arrival itself, and the arrival has no calendar.",
  "40": "In fact, the Hour will take them by surprise, leaving them stunned states the manner of the Hour's arrival, and the word \"surprise\" is the complete answer to every 'when.' The Hour will not come with an appointment that can be tracked or a warning that can be scheduled. It will come suddenly, the way a thief arrives in the night, and the verse says it will leave them stunned, the word for a person struck speechless and motionless by something too big to process. The same answer is given elsewhere in the Quran: \"It is too tremendous for the heavens and the earth and will only take you by surprise.\" The suddenness is not a detail of the Hour; it is the essence of it, and the verse makes the essence plain.\n\nThe second half of the verse removes the two hopes that follow surprise: \"So they will not be able to avert it, nor will it be delayed from them.\" Avert and delay, push it away and postpone it, are the two moves a person makes against an unwanted event, and the verse says both are impossible. There will be no bargaining, no second chance, and no rescheduling. The Hour is not a negotiation; it is a decree, and when it arrives, it arrives. The verse is the chapter's fullest answer to the mockers who asked \"when\": the answer is that the question itself assumed a control that does not exist. They asked for a date so they could prepare at their leisure, and the verse says the Hour does not consult their leisure. The only preparation is the one that happens now, before the surprise, and the chapter has been offering it since its opening line. The verse is also the chapter's answer to the idea that the Hour can be postponed by asking about it. Every question about its timing assumes that the questioner has time, and the verse says the assumption is the one thing that will certainly fail. The wise response to a surprise is not to ask for its schedule but to be ready for it at every hour.",
  "41": "\"˹Other˺ messengers had already been ridiculed before you ˹O Prophet˺, but those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule\" is the verse of consolation and warning in one sentence, and it is spoken to the Prophet in the middle of the mockery the chapter has been recording. The first half is the consolation: the mockery is not new, and it is not personal. Every messenger before him was ridiculed, and the Quran states the pattern elsewhere in almost identical words: \"˹Other˺ messengers had already been ridiculed before you ˹O Prophet˺, but those who mocked them were overtaken by what they used to ridicule.\" The mockery is part of the road, not a sign that the road is wrong, and the verse places the Prophet inside a long line of ridiculed messengers so that he never feels alone in it.\n\nThe second half is the warning, and it is aimed at the mockers: those who mocked were overtaken by the very thing they mocked. The word \"overtaken\" is the word of being surrounded and caught, and the thing that caught them was what they used to laugh at. The punishment they called a joke arrived and became their reality, and the verse states the law without softening it. The chapter has already shown the destroyed nations fleeing and being reaped, and this verse is the law drawn from those scenes: mockery does not cancel the threat; it accelerates the mocker toward it. The verse holds both messages in one sentence, the comfort for the messenger and the warning for the mocker, and the two are the same fact seen from two sides. What the mocker laughs at today is what will overtake him tomorrow, and what the messenger endures today is what his predecessors endured before their vindication.",
  "42": "Ask ˹them, O Prophet,˺ \"Who can defend you by day or by night against the Most Compassionate?\" is the question the Prophet is told to put to the mockers, and it is a question without an answer. Day and night cover all of time, and the question asks: who can defend you at any hour against God? The word \"defend\" is 'yakla'ukum,' to guard and shield, and the question searches for a protector who can stand between the people and their Lord. The answer is silence, because the gods they worshipped could not defend themselves, let alone their worshippers. The question is also aimed at the heart of their confidence: they felt safe in their wealth, their walls, and their gods, and the verse asks them to name the source of that safety, hour by hour, against the One who holds the hours.\n\nThe verse then records what they do instead of answering: \"Still they turn away from the remembrance of their Lord.\" The word \"remembrance\" is 'dhikr,' and it means both the reminder of the Quran and the remembering of God in the heart, and the verse says they turn away from both. The question is not answered, because it cannot be, and the turning away is their only response. The commentators note the structure of the verse: a question that exposes their helplessness, followed by the diagnosis of why they remain in it. They turn away from the very remembrance that would give them the protection they cannot find anywhere else. The verse is the chapter's most direct confrontation with the illusion of security, and the next verse will press the same point against their gods in the plainest terms. The question is also one that every believer can borrow. Safety is the deepest need a human being has, and the verse redirects the search for it away from the things that cannot provide it and toward the One who alone holds the hours. The person who asks this question honestly has already found half the answer.",
  "43": "\"Or do they have gods—other than Us—that can protect them?\" presses the question of the previous verse onto the gods themselves, and the answer is written into the question. The word \"or\" connects the two verses, as if offering the mockers a way out: if they cannot answer who defends them against God, then perhaps their gods can. The question then states what everyone knows: \"They cannot ˹even˺ protect themselves, nor will they be aided against Us.\" The gods they call on for protection cannot protect their own images from being broken, their own temples from falling, and their own names from being forgotten. A protector who cannot protect himself is not a protector at all, and the verse says the whole pantheon will have no help against God on the day when help is the only thing that matters.\n\nThe verse is the completion of the argument that began with the question about defense. The mockers were asked who defends them, and now their gods are examined, and the examination ends in the same place: there is no defense anywhere, from any being, at any hour. The verse also carries a quiet irony that the commentators note: the idols to whom the pagans prayed for protection were the very things that needed protection from the elements, the thieves, and the passage of time. The verse lets that irony stand without comment, because the facts speak. The chapter has now stripped the mockers of their two sources of confidence, their gods and their safety, and the next verse will turn to the third, the long years of prosperity that made them feel untouchable. Each verse in this passage removes one pillar of the illusion, and the illusion is almost down to the ground. The verse is also a mirror for anyone who leans on created things for security. Whatever a person calls on for protection, a god, a fortune, a government, or a circle of friends, the question of the verse applies to it: can it protect itself? If it cannot, then leaning on it against God is leaning on a shadow, and the verse asks the reader to notice the shadow before the day of no help arrives.",
  "44": "\"In fact, We have allowed enjoyment for these ˹Meccans˺ and their forefathers for such a long time ˹that they took it for granted˺\" explains the real reason behind their confidence, and the reason is a misread gift. The Meccans had enjoyed prosperity for generations, and the verse says the enjoyment was allowed by God, extended by His patience, and the extension had a side effect they never noticed: they took it for granted. The longer the enjoyment lasted, the more it felt like ownership instead of gift, and the more the gift felt like proof that no account was coming. The verse names the mechanism precisely: God's patience with a people becomes, in their own minds, the cancellation of His justice, and the delay that was meant as a chance to turn becomes a reason to keep turning away.\n\nThe second half of the verse points to the evidence they should have read: \"Do they not see that We gradually reduce ˹their˺ land from its borders?\" The gradual reduction of their territory, the losses at the edges of their domain, was the handwriting on the wall, and the verse asks whether they read it. The same question appears elsewhere: \"Do they not see that We gradually reduce their land from its borders? Allah decides—none can reverse His decision.\" The reduction was the first installment of the account, and they missed it. The verse closes with the question that turns their confidence inside out: \"Is it they who will then prevail?\" The answer is already known, and the question is asked only so that they can hear it. Prosperity is not victory, and the long enjoyment is not a verdict in their favour. It is a test that is running out, and the verse asks them to notice the edges before the middle follows.",
  "45": "Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"I warn you only by revelation\" states the source of the Prophet's warning, and the word \"only\" carries the whole verse. The Prophet does not warn from his own imagination, his own fear, or his own desire. He warns by revelation, and the warning he delivers is not his to adjust or postpone. The verse is the Quran's way of drawing the line between the messenger and the message: the messenger is a carrier, and the message is not his property. The mockers treated the warning as Muhammad's invention, something to be laughed off with the man who brought it, and the verse corrects the misunderstanding at its root. What is being said to them is not one man's opinion; it is revelation, and the difference between the two is the difference between a rumor and a fact.\n\nThe second half of the verse describes the listeners: \"But the deaf cannot hear the call when they are warned!\" The word \"deaf\" is the Quran's word for the heart that has closed itself, and the verse says plainly that such hearts do not hear the call. The same statement appears elsewhere: \"You certainly cannot make the dead hear ˹the truth˺. Nor can you make the deaf hear the call when they turn their backs and walk away.\" The verse is a boundary for the Prophet and a diagnosis for the mockers at once. The Prophet is told that his task is the delivery, not the forcing of hearing, and the mockers are told what their condition is: they are deaf to the one call that could save them. The verse closes the passage on the note the chapter has been sounding since its opening: the warning is real, the source is revelation, and the only ones who will not hear it are the ones who have chosen not to.",
  "46": "If they were touched by even a breath of your Lord’s torment, they would certainly cry, \"Woe to us! We have really been wrongdoers\" is the verse that measures the mockers by their own future, and the word \"breath\" is the key. The Arabic is 'nafhatun,' a single puff, a whiff, the smallest possible sample of the punishment, and the verse says that this smallest sample would be enough to produce the confession the mockers now refuse. One touch of the torment, no more than a breath, and the laughter of the previous verses would turn into the cry of woe. The verse then quotes the cry itself, and the words are exactly the words of the destroyed nations recorded earlier in the chapter: \"Woe to us! We have surely been wrongdoers.\" The chapter is showing that the mockers of Mecca are one breath away from joining the scene they were just told about.\n\nThe verse is the Quran's way of explaining the confidence of the deniers. They are not confident because the punishment is weak; they are confident because it is distant, and distance feels like safety. The verse removes the distance in the imagination. One breath of it, it says, and the whole posture collapses into confession. The commentators note the mercy folded into the warning: the verse is telling them what their own words will be, so that they can say them now, while the saying still counts. The confession of the verse is offered to them in advance, and the offer is the difference between the warning and the punishment. The breath has not touched them yet, and that is the only reason they can still choose which side of the verse they will stand on. The verse is also one of the chapter's gentlest warnings, because it speaks in the conditional. It does not say the torment has come; it says what would happen if it did, and the conditional leaves the reader outside the scene, able to hear the cry before it becomes their own.",
  "47": "\"We will set up the scales of justice on the Day of Judgment, so no soul will be wronged in the least\" opens the verse of the scales, and every word of it is chosen to answer the fear of injustice. The word for \"scales\" is 'mawazin,' and the word for \"justice\" is 'qist,' equity itself, so the scales are not merely instruments of measurement but instruments of fairness, and the verse says they will be set up on the Day of Judgment. The second phrase states the guarantee: \"no soul will be wronged in the least.\" The Arabic says not even by a thread, not even by the smallest amount, and the guarantee is absolute. Then the verse moves to the smallest unit the Arabs knew, the mustard seed: \"And ˹even˺ if a deed is the weight of a mustard seed, We will bring it forth.\" Nothing is too small for these scales. The tiniest good and the tiniest evil will both be produced, weighed, and answered, and the same standard appears elsewhere in the Quran's shortest chapter: \"So whoever does an atom’s weight of good will see it.\" And \"whoever does an atom’s weight of evil will see it.\"\n\nThe verse closes with the statement that makes the whole accounting possible: \"And sufficient are We as a ˹vigilant˺ Reckoner.\" The word for \"Reckoner\" is 'hasibin,' the One who counts and settles, and the verse says God's accounting is sufficient on its own. No witness needs to be called, no record needs to be double-checked, and no deed can be lost. The same promise is stated elsewhere: \"Indeed, Allah never wrongs ˹anyone˺—even by an atom’s weight.\" The verse is the believer's deepest comfort about the Day of Judgment: the scales exist, the measuring is exact, and the smallest deed survives. What a person does in secret, what they thought was forgotten, and what they themselves have forgotten, all of it is on the scale, because the Reckoner forgets nothing.",
  "48": "\"Indeed, We granted Moses and Aaron the standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺—a light and a reminder for the righteous\" introduces the chapter's turn from argument to story, and it begins with the gift given to Moses and Aaron. The word rendered \"standard\" is 'al-Furqan,' the Criterion, the thing that separates and distinguishes, and it is one of the names of the Scripture given to Moses. The Torah was a Furqan because it divided truth from falsehood, right from wrong, lawful from unlawful, and the verse names Aaron beside Moses because the two brothers carried the mission together, as the chapter of Ta-Ha described at length. The same gift is mentioned elsewhere with the same wording: \"And ˹remember˺ when We gave Moses the Scripture—the standard ˹to distinguish between right and wrong˺ that perhaps you would be ˹rightly˺ guided.\"\n\nThe verse then describes what the Furqan was for those who received it: \"a light and a reminder for the righteous\". A light, because it lets the one who holds it see where others stumble in the dark, and a reminder, because it keeps God present in the heart that reads it. The verse names the receivers as the righteous, and the next verse will describe who they are, but the important point is already made: the Scripture is not a burden or a museum piece. It is light and remembrance, and it was given to Moses and Aaron the way this Quran has been given to Muhammad, as the chapter will say in two verses. The stories that follow, the story of Abraham and the others, are all part of this chain: every messenger was given a standard, and the standards are all one light. The verse also prepares the reader for the pattern the chapter will repeat: God sends a standard, the people divide over it, and the standard itself remains as the witness. What was given to Moses and Aaron in their time has been given again, and the response of the pagans of Mecca to their Book is being measured against the response of the nations to theirs.",
  "49": "\"who are in awe of their Lord without seeing Him, and are fearful of the Hour\" defines the righteous for whom the previous verse said the Scripture is a light and a reminder, and the definition has two parts. The first is the unseen: \"in awe of their Lord without seeing Him\". The word for \"awe\" is 'khashyah,' the fear of reverence, the fear a person feels toward someone they love and respect so much that disappointing them is unbearable, and the verse says the righteous feel this toward God without ever having seen Him. Their awe is not produced by a visible presence; it is produced by knowledge and love, and that is why it is the highest form of awe. The same description appears elsewhere: \"who were in awe of the Most Compassionate without seeing ˹Him˺, and have come with a heart turning ˹only to Him˺.\" The unseen is the test: anyone can fear what they see, but only the believer fears what they know.\n\nThe second part is the Hour: \"and are fearful of the Hour.\" The word \"fearful\" here is 'mushfiqun,' the anxiety of someone who watches and guards, and it describes the righteous as people who live with the Day of Judgment in view. Their awe of God and their watchfulness of the Hour are the two sides of one posture: love that fears, and fear that watches. The verse is the Quran's compact portrait of the righteous, and it is placed here so that the reader can measure the coming stories against it. Abraham, Moses, and the rest of the prophets were exactly this: people who stood in awe of the unseen Lord and lived watchful of the Hour, and the chapter is about to show what that posture looks like in a human life.",
  "50": "\"And this ˹Quran˺ is a blessed reminder which We have revealed. Will you ˹pagans˺ then deny it?\" places the Quran beside the Scripture of Moses, and the comparison is the verse's argument. Moses and Aaron were given the Furqan, a light and a reminder, and now the verse says: and this Quran is a blessed reminder which We have revealed. The word \"blessed\" is 'mubarak,' full of lasting good, and the word \"reminder\" is the same 'dhikr' used for the Torah a moment earlier. The verse is saying to the pagans of Mecca: you accept, or half-accept, the Scripture of Moses as divine, so on what basis do you deny this Book, which is the same kind of gift, in your own language, revealed to a man from among yourselves? The comparison leaves the denial with no ground to stand on.\n\nThe verse then closes with the direct question: \"Will you ˹pagans˺ then deny it?\" The word \"then\" connects the question to the comparison, and the question is asked without waiting for an answer, because the answer is already visible in their behavior. The verse is also a comfort to the believers: the Book in their hands belongs to the same chain as the Book of Moses, and the chain is what the rest of the chapter will display. The stories of Abraham, Noah, David, Solomon, Job, and the others that follow are not a history lesson; they are the family tree of this Quran, and the verse has just introduced the tree. Every prophet in the coming pages received a reminder, and this is the same reminder, continuing. The question \"Will you ˹pagans˺ then deny it\" hangs over every story that follows, and each story is another witness against the denial. The verse also draws the line between the two responses a person can make to a revealed reminder: to receive it as a blessing and let it shape the life, or to deny it and live as if it had never come. The word 'blessed' is the invitation, and the question at the end is the warning, and between the two stands the choice the whole chapter has been describing.",
  "51": "“And indeed, We had granted Abraham sound judgment early on, for We knew him well ˹to be worthy of it˺.”” opens the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the grant: “And indeed, We had granted Abraham sound judgment early on, for We knew him well to be worthy of it,” or “wa laqad atayna Ibraheema rushdahu min qablu wa kunna bihi ‘alimeen,” and the commentators note the grant, for the Lord granted Abraham the sound judgment in the youth, and the verse is the record of the grant, the introduction of the story that the following verses unfold, and the commentators note the significance, for the \"rushd\" is the guidance, and the early grant is the favour, and the verse is the record of the grant, the introduction of the story of the idols. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the judgment is the guidance, and the grant is the favour, and the verse is the record of the grant, the introduction that the following verses unfold.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the grant, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own guidance. The believer is taught that the guidance is the gift, and the one whom the Lord grants is the one whose guidance the Lord values, and the believer who seeks the judgment is the believer whose seeking the Lord accepts, and the grant to Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the guidance is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the grant is the believer whose seeking the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gift of the guidance: Abraham was granted the judgment, and the believer who seeks the guidance is the believer whom the Lord grants, and the grant is the teaching that the judgment is the gift. The verse is the record of the grant, and the teaching of the guidance that the Lord grants.",
  "52": "“˹Remember˺ when he questioned his father and his people, “What are these statues to which you are so devoted?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two parts of the question. The verse records the question: “Remember when he questioned his father and his people, ‘What are these statues to which you are so devoted?’” or “ith qala li-abeehi wa qawmihi ma hathihi at-tamatheelu allatee antum laha ‘akifoon,” and the commentators note the question, for Abraham questioned the father and the people, and the question is the argument, the opening of the debate, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the argument of the reason, and the verse is the record of the question, the opening of the debate. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the argument, and the statues are the idols, and the verse is the record of the question, the argument that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reasoning. The believer is taught that the question is the argument, and the one who asks is the one whose reasoning the Lord values, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reasoning the Lord accepts, and the question of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the question is the thing that the truth uses, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose reasoning the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the question: Abraham questioned and exposed, and the believer who questions the false is the believer whose reasoning the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the argument is the thing that the truth uses. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the reasoning that the argument requires.",
  "53": "“They replied, “We found our forefathers worshipping them.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two elements of the reply. The verse records the reply: “We found our forefathers worshipping them,” or “qaloo wajadna aba’ana laha ‘abideen,” and the commentators note the reply, for the people appealed to the forefathers, and the reply is the confession, the declaration that the only ground of the worship is the inheritance, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the appeal to the forefathers is the weakness, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the inheritance, and the confession is the weakness, and the verse is the record of the reply, the confession that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the reply, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the inheritance is not the proof, and the one who appeals to the forefathers is the one whose appeal the reason refutes, and the believer who seeks the proof is the believer whose seeking the Lord values, and the reply of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the inheritance is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose discernment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the weakness of the inheritance: the people appealed to the fathers, and the believer who seeks the proof is the believer whose seeking the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the inheritance is not the evidence. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the discernment that the seeking requires.",
  "54": "“He responded, “Indeed, you and your forefathers have been clearly astray.”” records the verdict, and the commentators note the two parts of the verdict. The verse records the verdict: “Indeed, you and your forefathers have been clearly astray,” or “qala laqad kuntum antum wa aba’ukum fee dalalin mubeen,” and the commentators note the verdict, for Abraham declared the misguidance, and the verdict is the truth, the declaration that the inheritance was the error, and the verse is the record of the verdict, the truth that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the verdict is the correction, and the verse is the record of the verdict, the correction of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the verdict is the correction, and the misguidance is the error, and the verse is the record of the verdict, the correction that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the verdict, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own courage. The believer is taught that the declaration of the truth is the courage, and the one who declares the error is the one whose courage the Lord values, and the believer who speaks the truth is the believer whose courage the Lord accepts, and the verdict of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the believer must speak, and the believer who reads the verdict is the believer whose courage the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the courage of the truth: Abraham declared the error, and the believer who speaks the truth is the believer whose courage the Lord values, and the verdict is the teaching that the truth is the thing that the believer must declare. The verse is the record of the verdict, and the teaching of the courage that the truth requires.",
  "55": "“They asked, “Have you come to us with the truth, or is this a joke?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the question. The verse records the question: “Have you come to us with the truth, or is this a joke?” or “qaloo a-ji’tana bil-haqqi am anta mina al-la‘ibeen,” and the commentators note the question, for the people asked whether Abraham was serious or jesting, and the question is the dismissal, the refusal to take the truth, and the verse is the record of the question, the dismissal that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the question is the mockery, and the verse is the record of the question, the dismissal of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the dismissal, and the mockery is the refusal, and the verse is the record of the question, the dismissal that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own seriousness. The believer is taught that the dismissal of the truth is the mockery, and the one who jests is the one whose jest the Lord weighs, and the believer who is serious is the believer whose seriousness the Lord values, and the question of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the mockery is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose seriousness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the seriousness of the truth: the people mocked and were answered, and the believer who is serious is the believer whose seriousness the Lord values, and the question is the teaching that the mockery is the thing that the falsehood uses. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the seriousness that the truth requires.",
  "56": "“He replied, “In fact, your Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth, Who created them ˹both˺. And to that I bear witness.”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “In fact, your Lord is the Lord of the heavens and the earth, Who created them both. And to that I bear witness,” or “qala bal rabbukum rabbu as-samawati wal-ardi allathee fatarahunna wa ana ‘ala thalikum mina ash-shahideen,” and the commentators note the reply, for Abraham declared the Lord and the witness, and the reply is the truth, the declaration of the oneness, and the verse is the record of the reply, the truth that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the declaration of the Lord is the argument, and the witness is the commitment, and the verse is the record of the reply, the truth of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the declaration is the argument, and the witness is the commitment, and the verse is the record of the reply, the truth that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the witness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own faith. The believer is taught that the witness of the truth is the commitment, and the one who bears witness is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who declares the oneness is the believer whose faith the Lord accepts, and the reply of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the witness is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose faith the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the witness of the truth: Abraham bore witness, and the believer who declares the oneness is the believer whose faith the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the witness is the commitment. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the faith that the witness establishes.",
  "57": "“˹Then he said to himself,˺ “By Allah! I will surely plot against your idols after you have turned your backs and gone away.”” records the oath, and the commentators note the two parts of the oath. The verse records the oath: “Then he said to himself, ‘By Allah! I will surely plot against your idols after you have turned your backs and gone away,’” or “wa tallahi la-akeedanna asnamakum ba‘da an tuwalloo mudbireen,” and the commentators note the oath, for Abraham swore the plot, and the oath is the resolve, the plan of the destruction of the idols, and the verse is the record of the oath, the resolve that the following verse fulfills, and the commentators note the significance, for the oath is the commitment, and the plot is the plan, and the verse is the record of the oath, the resolve of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the oath is the commitment, and the plot is the plan, and the verse is the record of the oath, the resolve that the following verse fulfills.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the resolve, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own courage. The believer is taught that the resolve against the false is the courage, and the one who plans the correction is the one whose courage the Lord values, and the believer who acts against the false is the believer whose courage the Lord accepts, and the oath of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the resolve is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the oath is the believer whose courage the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the resolve of the truth: Abraham swore and acted, and the believer who acts against the false is the believer whose courage the Lord values, and the oath is the teaching that the resolve is the beginning of the action. The verse is the record of the oath, and the teaching of the courage that the resolve requires.",
  "58": "“So he smashed them into pieces, except the biggest of them, so they might turn to it ˹for answers˺.”” records the smashing, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the smashing: “So he smashed them into pieces, except the biggest of them, so they might turn to it for answers,” or “fa-ja‘alahum juthathan illa kabeeran lahum la‘allahum ilayhi yarji‘oon,” and the commentators note the smashing, for Abraham smashed the idols and left the biggest, and the verse is the record of the smashing, the plan that the following verse shows the effect of, and the commentators note the significance, for the leaving of the biggest is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the smashing, the plan of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the smashing is the action, and the leaving is the wisdom, and the verse is the record of the smashing, the plan that the following verse displays.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the wisdom, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the correction of the false is the wisdom, and the one who leaves the evidence is the one whose wisdom the Lord values, and the believer who acts with the wisdom is the believer whose discernment the Lord accepts, and the smashing of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the believer must use, and the believer who reads the smashing is the believer whose wisdom the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the action: Abraham smashed and left, and the believer who acts with the wisdom is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the smashing is the teaching that the wisdom is the companion of the action. The verse is the record of the smashing, and the teaching of the wisdom that the correction requires.",
  "59": "“They protested, “Who dared do this to our gods? It must be an evildoer!”” records the protest, and the commentators note the two parts of the protest. The verse records the protest: “Who dared do this to our gods? It must be an evildoer!” or “qaloo man fa‘ala hatha bi-alihatina innahu la-mina az-zalimeen,” and the commentators note the protest, for the people protested the smashing, and the protest is the accusation, the charge of the wrongdoing, and the verse is the record of the protest, the accusation that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the protest is the irony, the charge of the wrong against the one who did the right, and the verse is the record of the protest, the accusation of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the protest is the accusation, and the irony is the charge, and the verse is the record of the protest, the accusation that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the protest, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the accusation of the wrong is the thing that the truth faces, and the one who is charged is the one whose truth the Lord exposes, and the believer who bears the accusation is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the protest of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the charge is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the protest is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the accused: Abraham was charged and vindicated, and the believer who bears the accusation is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the protest is the teaching that the charge is the thing that the truth faces. The verse is the record of the protest, and the teaching of the patience that the accusation requires.",
  "60": "“Some said, “We heard a young man, called Abraham, speaking ˹ill˺ of them.”” records the testimony, and the commentators note the two parts of the testimony. The verse records the testimony: “Some said, ‘We heard a young man, called Abraham, speaking ill of them,’” or “qaloo sami‘na fatan yathkuruhum yuqalu lahu Ibraheem,” and the commentators note the testimony, for the people remembered Abraham, and the testimony is the witness, the identification of the smasher, and the verse is the record of the testimony, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the mention of the \"fatan\" — the young man — is the description, and the verse is the record of the testimony, the identification of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the testimony is the witness, and the young man is the description, and the verse is the record of the testimony, the identification that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the testimony, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reputation. The believer is taught that the reputation of the truth is the thing that the falsehood remembers, and the one who is known is the one whose truth the Lord exposes, and the believer who speaks the truth is the believer whose reputation the Lord raises, and the testimony of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the truth is the thing that the falsehood remembers, and the believer who reads the testimony is the believer whose courage the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reputation of the truth: Abraham was remembered, and the believer who speaks the truth is the believer whose reputation the Lord raises, and the testimony is the teaching that the truth is the thing that the falsehood cannot forget. The verse is the record of the testimony, and the teaching of the reputation that the truth builds.",
  "61": "“They demanded, “Bring him before the eyes of the people, so that they may witness ˹his trial˺.”” records the demand, and the commentators note the two elements of the demand. The verse records the demand: “Bring him before the eyes of the people, so that they may witness his trial,” or “qaloo fa’too bihi ‘ala a‘yuni an-nasi la‘allahum yashhadoon,” and the commentators note the demand, for the people demanded the bringing of Abraham, and the demand is the public trial, and the verse is the record of the demand, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the public trial is the stage, and the verse is the record of the demand, the stage that the truth would use. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the demand is the public, and the trial is the stage, and the verse is the record of the demand, the stage that the following verses display.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the demand, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own confidence. The believer is taught that the public trial is the stage, and the one who is brought is the one whose truth the Lord displays, and the believer who faces the public is the believer whose confidence the Lord values, and the demand of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the public is the arena of the truth, and the believer who reads the demand is the believer whose confidence the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the arena of the truth: Abraham was brought and displayed, and the believer who faces the public is the believer whose truth the Lord displays, and the demand is the teaching that the public is the arena of the truth. The verse is the record of the demand, and the teaching of the confidence that the public requires.",
  "62": "“They asked, “Was it you who did this to our gods, O Abraham?”” records the question, and the commentators note the two elements of the question. The verse records the question: “Was it you who did this to our gods, O Abraham?” or “qaloo a-anta fa‘alta hatha bi-alihatina ya Ibraheem,” and the commentators note the question, for the people asked the direct question, and the question is the trial, the opening of the dialogue, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the direct question is the opportunity, and the verse is the record of the question, the opportunity that the truth would use. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the question is the trial, and the opportunity is the answer, and the verse is the record of the question, the step that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the question, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own answer. The believer is taught that the question of the falsehood is the opportunity, and the one who is asked is the one whose answer the Lord guides, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord values, and the question of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the question is the door of the answer, and the believer who reads the question is the believer whose answer the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the opportunity of the question: Abraham was asked and answered, and the believer who answers with the truth is the believer whose answer the Lord guides, and the question is the teaching that the inquiry is the door of the truth. The verse is the record of the question, and the teaching of the answer that the question invites.",
  "63": "“He replied ˹sarcastically˺, “No, this one—the biggest of them—did it! So ask them, if they can talk!”” records the reply, and the commentators note the two parts of the reply. The verse records the reply: “No, this one—the biggest of them—did it! So ask them, if they can talk!” or “qala bal fa‘alahu kabeeruhum hatha fas’aloohum in kanoo yantiqoon,” and the commentators note the reply, for Abraham attributed the deed to the biggest, and the reply is the argument, the sarcasm that exposes the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the reply, the argument that the following verse shows the effect of, and the commentators note the significance, for the reply is the wisdom, the demonstration of the absurdity, and the verse is the record of the reply, the argument of Abraham. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the reply is the sarcasm, and the argument is the absurdity, and the verse is the record of the reply, the argument that the following verse shows the effect of.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the argument, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own discernment. The believer is taught that the argument of the absurdity is the wisdom, and the one who exposes the false is the one whose discernment the Lord values, and the believer who reasons with the wisdom is the believer whose discernment the Lord accepts, and the reply of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the absurdity is the thing that the truth exposes, and the believer who reads the reply is the believer whose discernment the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the wisdom of the argument: Abraham exposed the absurdity, and the believer who reasons with the wisdom is the believer whose discernment the Lord values, and the reply is the teaching that the absurdity is the thing that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the reply, and the teaching of the discernment that the argument requires.",
  "64": "“So they came back to their senses, saying ˹to one another˺, “You yourselves are truly the wrongdoers!”” records the awakening, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the awakening: “So they came back to their senses, saying to one another, ‘You yourselves are truly the wrongdoers!’” or “fa-raja‘oo ila anfusihim fa-qaloo innakum antumu az-zalimoon,” and the commentators note the awakening, for the people came to their senses and confessed the wrong, and the verse is the record of the awakening, the first effect of the argument, and the commentators note the significance, for the confession is the awakening, and the verse is the record of the awakening, the first effect of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the awakening is the confession, and the confession is the effect, and the verse is the record of the awakening, the first effect that the following verse shows the reversal of.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the awakening, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own reflection. The believer is taught that the truth awakens the reason, and the one who reflects is the one whose confession the Lord values, and the believer who reasons is the believer whose reflection the Lord accepts, and the awakening of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the truth produces, and the believer who reads the awakening is the believer whose reflection the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the effect of the truth: the people awakened and confessed, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose reflection the Lord values, and the awakening is the teaching that the truth awakens the reason. The verse is the record of the awakening, and the teaching of the reflection that the truth produces.",
  "65": "“Then they ˹quickly˺ regressed to their ˹original˺ mind-set, ˹arguing,˺ “You already know that those ˹idols˺ cannot talk.”” records the regression, and the commentators note the two parts of the regression. The verse records the regression: “Then they quickly regressed to their original mind-set, arguing, ‘You already know that those idols cannot talk,’” or “thumma nukisoo ‘ala ru’oosihim laqad ‘alimta ma ha’ula’i yantiqoon,” and the commentators note the regression, for the people returned to the stubbornness, and the regression is the reversal, the return to the denial, and the verse is the record of the regression, the reversal that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the regression is the stubbornness, and the verse is the record of the regression, the reversal of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the regression is the reversal, and the stubbornness is the denial, and the verse is the record of the regression, the reversal that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the regression, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own steadfastness. The believer is taught that the regression is the thing that the falsehood does, and the one who returns to the denial is the one whose reversal the Lord weighs, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord values, and the regression of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the reversal is the thing that the believer must avoid, and the believer who reads the regression is the believer whose steadfastness the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the danger of the reversal: the people regressed and were rebuked, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord values, and the regression is the teaching that the reversal is the thing that the believer must avoid. The verse is the record of the regression, and the teaching of the steadfastness that the truth requires.",
  "66": "“He rebuked ˹them˺, “Do you then worship—instead of Allah—what can neither benefit nor harm you in any way?”” records the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the rebuke. The verse records the rebuke: “Do you then worship—instead of Allah—what can neither benefit nor harm you in any way?” or “qala a-fa-ta‘budoona min dooni Allahi ma la yanfa‘ukum shay’an wa la yadurrukum,” and the commentators note the rebuke, for Abraham rebuked the worship, and the rebuke is the argument, the declaration that the idols cannot benefit or harm, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the argument that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the rebuke is the exposure, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the argument of the truth. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the rebuke is the exposure, and the argument is the truth, and the verse is the record of the rebuke, the argument that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the rebuke, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the rebuke of the false is the argument, and the one who exposes is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who reasons is the believer whose certainty the Lord accepts, and the rebuke of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the false is the thing that the truth exposes, and the believer who reads the rebuke is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the argument of the truth: Abraham exposed the false, and the believer who reasons is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the rebuke is the teaching that the false is the thing that the truth exposes. The verse is the record of the rebuke, and the teaching of the certainty that the argument produces.",
  "67": "“Shame on you and whatever you worship instead of Allah! Do you not have any sense?”” completes the rebuke, and the commentators note the two parts of the completion. The verse completes the rebuke: “Shame on you and whatever you worship instead of Allah! Do you not have any sense?” or “uffin lakum wa lima ta‘budoona min dooni Allahi afala ta‘qiloon,” and the commentators note the completion, for Abraham declared the shame and appealed to the intellect, and the completion is the rebuke, the declaration of the absurdity, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rebuke that the following verse answers with the fire, and the commentators note the significance, for the shame is the verdict, and the appeal is the question, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rebuke of the people. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the shame is the verdict, and the appeal is the question, and the verse is the record of the completion, the rebuke that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the completion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own certainty. The believer is taught that the appeal to the intellect is the argument, and the one who reasons is the one whose certainty the Lord values, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose certainty the Lord accepts, and the completion of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the reflection is the thing that the truth seeks, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose certainty the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the appeal of the truth: Abraham appealed to the intellect, and the believer who reflects is the believer whose certainty the Lord values, and the completion is the teaching that the reflection is the thing that the truth seeks. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the certainty that the reflection produces.",
  "68": "“They concluded, “Burn him up to avenge your gods, if you must act.”” records the conclusion, and the commentators note the two parts of the conclusion. The verse records the conclusion: “Burn him up to avenge your gods, if you must act,” or “qaloo harriqoohu wansuroo alihatakum in kuntum fa‘ileen,” and the commentators note the conclusion, for the people resolved the burning, and the conclusion is the violence, the answer of the falsehood, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the violence that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the burning is the violence, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the violence of the falsehood. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the conclusion is the violence, and the burning is the punishment, and the verse is the record of the conclusion, the violence that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the conclusion, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the violence of the falsehood is the trial, and the one who is threatened is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the conclusion of the people is the teaching, the declaration that the violence is the thing that the falsehood uses, and the believer who reads the conclusion is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the violence of the falsehood: Abraham was sentenced and saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the conclusion is the teaching that the violence is the thing that the truth withstands. The verse is the record of the conclusion, and the teaching of the trust that the violence requires.",
  "69": "“We ordered, “O fire! Be cool and safe for Abraham!”” records the command, and the commentators note the two parts of the command. The verse records the command: “O fire! Be cool and safe for Abraham!” or “qulna ya naru koonee bardan wa salaman ‘ala Ibraheem,” and the commentators note the command, for the Lord commanded the fire, and the command is the miracle, the cooling of the fire, and the verse is the record of the command, the miracle that the following verse reflects upon, and the commentators note the significance, for the command to the fire is the power, and the cooling is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the command, the miracle of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the command is the power, and the cooling is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the command, the miracle that the following verse reflects upon.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the command, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who faces the fire is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the command to the fire is the teaching, the declaration that the power of the Lord is the complete, and the believer who reads the command is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the fire was cooled, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the command is the teaching that the power is the thing that the believer must trust. The verse is the record of the command, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "70": "“They had sought to harm him, but We made them the worst losers.”” records the result, and the commentators note the two parts of the result. The verse records the result: “They had sought to harm him, but We made them the worst losers,” or “wa aradoo bihi kaydan fa-ja‘alnahumu al-akhsareen,” and the commentators note the result, for the Lord turned the plot, and the verse is the record of the result, the frustration of the scheme, and the commentators note the significance, for the plot is the thing that the Lord frustrates, and the verse is the record of the result, the frustration that the following verse completes. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the result is the frustration, and the losers are the plotters, and the verse is the record of the result, the frustration that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the result, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the plot of the falsehood is the thing that the Lord frustrates, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose trust the Lord confirms, and the believer who faces the scheme is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the result of the verse is the teaching, the declaration that the plot is the thing that the Lord turns, and the believer who reads the result is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the frustration of the plot: the people lost and Abraham was saved, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the result is the teaching that the plot is the thing that the Lord turns. The verse is the record of the result, and the teaching of the trust that the frustration produces.",
  "71": "“Then We delivered him, along with Lot, to the land We had showered with blessings for all people.”” records the deliverance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the deliverance: “Then We delivered him, along with Lot, to the land We had showered with blessings for all people,” or “wa najjaynahu wa Lootan ila al-ardi allatee barakna feeha lil-‘alameen,” and the commentators note the deliverance, for the Lord delivered Abraham and Lot to the blessed land, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the migration that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the blessed land is the Syria, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the migration of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the deliverance is the migration, and the blessed land is the Syria, and the verse is the record of the deliverance, the migration that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the deliverance of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is delivered is the one whose trust the Lord rewards, and the believer who migrates for the faith is the believer whose migration the Lord values, and the deliverance of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the migration is the path of the mercy, and the believer who reads the deliverance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the migration: Abraham was delivered, and the believer who migrates for the faith is the believer whose migration the Lord values, and the deliverance is the teaching that the migration is the path of the mercy. The verse is the record of the deliverance, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "72": "“And We blessed him with Isaac ˹as a son˺ and Jacob ˹as a grandson˺, as an additional favour—making all of them righteous.”” records the blessing, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the blessing: “And We blessed him with Isaac as a son and Jacob as a grandson, as an additional favour—making all of them righteous,” or “wa wahabna lahu Ishaqa wa Ya‘qooba nafilatan wa kullan ja‘alna saliheen,” and the commentators note the blessing, for the Lord granted Isaac and Jacob, and the verse is the record of the blessing, the grant that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the grant of the two is the reward, and the righteousness is the description, and the verse is the record of the blessing, the grant of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the grant is the reward, and the righteousness is the description, and the verse is the record of the blessing, the grant that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the blessing, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the blessing of the Lord is the reward, and the one whom the Lord grants is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the believer who hopes the reward is the believer whose hope the Lord accepts, and the blessing of Abraham is the teaching, the declaration that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives, and the believer who reads the blessing is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the Lord: Abraham was granted, and the believer who hopes the reward is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the blessing is the teaching that the grant is the thing that the Lord gives. The verse is the record of the blessing, and the teaching of the hope that the reward carries.",
  "73": "“We ˹also˺ made them leaders, guiding by Our command, and inspired them to do good deeds, establish prayer, and pay alms-tax. And they were devoted to Our worship.”” records the leadership, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the leadership: “We also made them leaders, guiding by Our command, and inspired them to do good deeds, establish prayer, and pay alms-tax. And they were devoted to Our worship,” or “wa ja‘alnahum a’immatan yahdoona bi-amrina wa awhayna ilayhim fi‘la al-khayrati wa iqama as-salati wa eeta’a az-zakati wa kanoo lana ‘abideen,” and the commentators note the leadership, for the Lord made them leaders, and the verse is the record of the leadership, the grant that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the leadership is the trust, and the inspiration is the guidance, and the verse is the record of the leadership, the grant of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the leadership is the trust, and the guidance is the command, and the verse is the record of the leadership, the grant that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the leadership, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own responsibility. The believer is taught that the leadership is the trust, and the one who leads is the one whose leadership the Lord values, and the believer who guides to the truth is the believer whose guidance the Lord accepts, and the leadership of the prophets is the teaching, the declaration that the guidance is the thing that the believer must offer, and the believer who reads the leadership is the believer whose responsibility the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the leadership: the prophets led and guided, and the believer who guides to the truth is the believer whose leadership the Lord values, and the leadership is the teaching that the guidance is the trust. The verse is the record of the leadership, and the teaching of the responsibility that the guidance requires.",
  "74": "“And to Lot We gave wisdom and knowledge, and delivered him from the society engrossed in shameful practices. They were certainly an evil, rebellious people.”” records the grant to Lot, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the grant: “And to Lot We gave wisdom and knowledge, and delivered him from the society engrossed in shameful practices. They were certainly an evil, rebellious people,” or “wa Lootan ataynahu hukman wa ‘ilman wa najjaynahu mina al-qaryati allatee kanat ta‘malu al-khaba’itha innahum kanoo qawma saw’in fasiqeen,” and the commentators note the grant, for the Lord granted Lot the wisdom and the knowledge, and the deliverance, and the verse is the record of the grant, the mercy that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the deliverance is the rescue, and the description is the verdict, and the verse is the record of the grant, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the grant is the mercy, and the deliverance is the rescue, and the verse is the record of the grant, the mercy that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the deliverance, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the deliverance of the Lord is the mercy, and the one who is rescued is the one whose faith the Lord values, and the believer who holds the purity is the believer whose purity the Lord accepts, and the deliverance of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the purity is the thing that the Lord rescues, and the believer who reads the deliverance is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mercy of the rescue: Lot was delivered, and the believer who holds the purity is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the deliverance is the teaching that the purity is the thing that the Lord rescues. The verse is the record of the grant, and the teaching of the hope that the deliverance carries.",
  "75": "“And We admitted him into Our mercy, ˹for˺ he was truly one of the righteous.”” completes the account, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: “And We admitted him into Our mercy, for he was truly one of the righteous,” or “wa adkhalnahu fee rahmatina innahu mina as-saliheen,” and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord admitted Lot into the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the admission is the reward, and the righteousness is the condition, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the admission is the reward, and the righteousness is the condition, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mercy, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the mercy of the Lord is for the righteous, and the one who lives the righteousness is the one whom the Lord admits, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the completion of Lot is the teaching, the declaration that the righteousness is the path of the mercy, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the righteousness: Lot was admitted, and the believer who lives the righteousness is the believer whom the Lord admits, and the completion is the teaching that the righteousness is the path of the mercy. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "76": "“And ˹remember˺ when Noah had cried out to Us earlier, so We responded to him and delivered him and his family from the great distress.”” records Noah, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Noah: “And remember when Noah had cried out to Us earlier, so We responded to him and delivered him and his family from the great distress,” or “wa Noohan ith nada min qablu fastajabna lahu fa-najjaynahu wa ahlahu mina al-karbi al-‘azeem,” and the commentators note the cry, for Noah cried and the Lord answered, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the cry is the prayer, and the answer is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the cry is the prayer, and the answer is the mercy, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the believer is the answered, and the one who cries is the one whom the Lord hears, and the believer who calls the Lord is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the answer to Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the prayer is the thing that the Lord answers, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the prayer: Noah cried and was answered, and the believer who calls the Lord is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the answer is the teaching that the prayer is the thing that the Lord hears. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the hope that the prayer carries.",
  "77": "“And We made him prevail over those who had rejected Our signs. They were truly an evil people, so We drowned them all.”” completes the account of Noah, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the account: “And We made him prevail over those who had rejected Our signs. They were truly an evil people, so We drowned them all,” or “wa nasarnahu mina al-qawmi allatheena kaththaboo bi-ayatina innahum kanoo qawma saw’in fa-aghraqnahum ajma‘een,” and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord granted Noah the victory and drowned the deniers, and the verse is the record of the completion, the victory that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the victory is the support, and the drowning is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the victory of Noah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the victory is the support, and the drowning is the judgment, and the verse is the record of the completion, the victory that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the victory, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the victory of the Lord is the support, and the one who trusts the Lord is the one whose victory the Lord grants, and the believer who holds the truth is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the victory of Noah is the teaching, the declaration that the truth prevails, and the believer who reads the victory is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the victory of the truth: Noah prevailed and the deniers drowned, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose victory the Lord grants, and the victory is the teaching that the truth prevails. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the trust that the victory produces.",
  "78": "“And ˹remember˺ when David and Solomon passed judgment regarding the crops ruined ˹at night˺ by someone’s sheep, and We were witness to their judgments.”” records the judgment, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the judgment: “And remember when David and Solomon passed judgment regarding the crops ruined at night by someone’s sheep, and We were witness to their judgments,” or “wa Dawooda wa Sulaymana ith yahkumani fi al-harthi ith nafashat feehi ghanamu al-qawmi wa kunna li-hukmihim shahideen,” and the commentators note the judgment, for David and Solomon judged the case of the sheep and the crops, and the verse is the record of the judgment, the case that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the two judgments are the wisdom, and the witness is the knowledge, and the verse is the record of the judgment, the case of the two prophets. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the judgment is the case, and the witness is the knowledge, and the verse is the record of the judgment, the case that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the judgment, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own justice. The believer is taught that the judgment is the trust, and the one who judges is the one whose justice the Lord values, and the believer who judges with the fairness is the believer whose justice the Lord accepts, and the judgment of the two prophets is the teaching, the declaration that the justice is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the judgment is the believer whose justice the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the trust of the judgment: the two prophets judged and the Lord witnessed, and the believer who judges with the fairness is the believer whose justice the Lord values, and the judgment is the teaching that the justice is the trust. The verse is the record of the judgment, and the teaching of the justice that the judgment requires.",
  "79": "“We guided ˹young˺ Solomon to a fairer settlement, and granted each of them wisdom and knowledge. We subjected the mountains as well as the birds to hymn ˹Our praises˺ along with David. It is We Who did ˹it all˺.”” records the guidance, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the guidance: “We guided young Solomon to a fairer settlement, and granted each of them wisdom and knowledge. We subjected the mountains as well as the birds to hymn Our praises along with David. It is We Who did it all,” or “fa-fahhamnaha Sulaymana wa kullan atayna hukman wa ‘ilman wa sakhkharna ma‘a Dawooda al-jibala yusabbihna wat-tayra wa kunna fa‘ileen,” and the commentators note the guidance, for the Lord guided Solomon to the fairer settlement, and the verse is the record of the guidance, the wisdom that the following verse develops, and the commentators note the significance, for the guidance is the wisdom, and the subjection is the power, and the verse is the record of the guidance, the wisdom of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the guidance is the wisdom, and the subjection is the power, and the verse is the record of the guidance, the wisdom that the following verse develops.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the wisdom, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own seeking. The believer is taught that the wisdom is the gift, and the one whom the Lord guides is the one whose judgment the Lord values, and the believer who seeks the wisdom is the believer whose seeking the Lord accepts, and the guidance of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the wisdom is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the guidance is the believer whose seeking the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gift of the wisdom: Solomon was guided, and the believer who seeks the wisdom is the believer whom the Lord grants, and the guidance is the teaching that the wisdom is the gift. The verse is the record of the guidance, and the teaching of the wisdom that the seeking requires.",
  "80": "“We taught him the art of making body armour to protect you in battle. Will you then be grateful?”” records the craft, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the craft: “We taught him the art of making body armour to protect you in battle. Will you then be grateful?” or “wa ‘allamnahu san‘ata laboosin lakum li-tuhsinakum min ba’sikum fa-hal antum shakiroon,” and the commentators note the craft, for the Lord taught David the making of the armour, and the verse is the record of the craft, the gift that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the craft is the gift, and the protection is the benefit, and the verse is the record of the craft, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the craft is the gift, and the protection is the benefit, and the verse is the record of the craft, the gift that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the craft, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own gratitude. The believer is taught that the craft of the believer is the gift, and the one who is taught is the one whose gratitude the Lord values, and the believer who thanks the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord accepts, and the craft of David is the teaching, the declaration that the skill is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the craft is the believer whose gratitude the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the gratitude of the gift: David was taught and asked the gratitude, and the believer who thanks the gift is the believer whose gratitude the Lord values, and the craft is the teaching that the skill is the gift. The verse is the record of the craft, and the teaching of the gratitude that the gift requires.",
  "81": "“And to Solomon We subjected the raging winds, blowing by his command to the land We had showered with blessings. It is We Who know everything.”” records the wind, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the wind: “And to Solomon We subjected the raging winds, blowing by his command to the land We had showered with blessings. It is We Who know everything,” or “wa li-Sulaymana ar-reeha ‘asifatan tajree bi-amrihi ila al-ardi allatee barakna feeha wa kunna bi-kulli shay’in ‘alimeen,” and the commentators note the wind, for the Lord subjected the wind to Solomon, and the verse is the record of the wind, the gift that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the subjection is the power, and the knowledge is the attribute, and the verse is the record of the wind, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the subjection is the power, and the knowledge is the attribute, and the verse is the record of the wind, the gift that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the power, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one whom the Lord subjects is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the wind of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the wind is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the wind was subjected, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the wind is the teaching that the power is the gift. The verse is the record of the wind, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "82": "“And ˹We subjected˺ some jinn that dived for him, and performed other duties. It is We Who kept them in check.”” records the jinn, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the jinn: “And We subjected some jinn that dived for him, and performed other duties. It is We Who kept them in check,” or “wa mina ash-shayateeni man yaghoosoona lahu wa ya‘maloona ‘amalan doona thalika wa kunna lahum hafizeen,” and the commentators note the jinn, for the Lord subjected the jinn to Solomon, and the verse is the record of the jinn, the gift that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the subjection is the power, and the keeping is the guard, and the verse is the record of the jinn, the gift of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the subjection is the power, and the keeping is the guard, and the verse is the record of the jinn, the gift that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the power, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own trust. The believer is taught that the power of the Lord is the thing that the believer trusts, and the one whom the Lord subjects is the one whose trust the Lord values, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the jinn of Solomon is the teaching, the declaration that the power is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the jinn is the believer whose trust the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the power of the Lord: the jinn were subjected, and the believer who trusts the power is the believer whose trust the Lord confirms, and the jinn is the teaching that the power is the gift. The verse is the record of the jinn, and the teaching of the trust that the power produces.",
  "83": "“And ˹remember˺ when Job cried out to his Lord, “I have been touched with adversity, and You are the Most Merciful of the merciful.”” records Job, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Job: “And remember when Job cried out to his Lord, ‘I have been touched with adversity, and You are the Most Merciful of the merciful,’” or “wa Ayyooba ith nada rabbahu annee massaniya ad-durru wa anta arhamu ar-rahimeen,” and the commentators note the cry, for Job cried the adversity and the praise, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the cry is the patience, and the praise is the hope, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer of the patient. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the cry is the patience, and the praise is the hope, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the patience, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own endurance. The believer is taught that the patience of the trial is the thing that the Lord values, and the one who cries with the praise is the one whose patience the Lord rewards, and the believer who endures the adversity is the believer whose patience the Lord accepts, and the cry of Job is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the cry is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the patience of the trial: Job cried and was answered, and the believer who endures the adversity is the believer whose patience the Lord rewards, and the cry is the teaching that the patience is the path of the answer. The verse is the record of the cry, and the teaching of the patience that the trial requires.",
  "84": "“So We answered his prayer and removed his adversity, and gave him back his family, twice as many, as a mercy from Us and a lesson for the ˹devoted˺ worshippers.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “So We answered his prayer and removed his adversity, and gave him back his family, twice as many, as a mercy from Us and a lesson for the devoted worshippers,” or “fastajabna lahu fa-kashafna ma bihi min durrin wa ataynahu ahlahu wa mithlahum ma‘ahum rahmatan min ‘indina wa thikra lil-‘abideen,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord removed the adversity and restored the family, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the restoration is the mercy, and the lesson is the reminder, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the answer is the mercy, and the restoration is the reward, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the patient is the answered, and the one who endures is the one whose adversity the Lord removes, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the answer to Job is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the path of the mercy, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the patience: Job was restored, and the believer who endures the adversity is the believer whose adversity the Lord removes, and the answer is the teaching that the patience is the thing that the Lord rewards. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "85": "“And ˹remember˺ Ishmael, Enoch, and Ⱬul-Kifl. They were all steadfast.”” records the three, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records the three: “And remember Ishmael, Enoch, and Dhul-Kifl. They were all steadfast,” or “wa Isma‘eela wa Idreesa wa tha al-kifli kullun mina as-sabireen,” and the commentators note the three, for the verse mentions Ishmael and Enoch and Dhul-Kifl, and the description is the steadfastness, and the verse is the record of the three, the mention that the following verse completes, and the commentators note the significance, for the steadfastness is the description, and the verse is the record of the three, the mention of the patient. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the three are the prophets, and the steadfastness is the description, and the verse is the record of the three, the mention that the following verse completes.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the steadfastness, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own patience. The believer is taught that the steadfastness is the mark of the prophets, and the one who endures is the one whose patience the Lord values, and the believer who holds the patience is the believer whose steadfastness the Lord accepts, and the mention of the three is the teaching, the declaration that the patience is the thing that the believer must hold, and the believer who reads the mention is the believer whose patience the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the mark of the steadfast: the three were the patient, and the believer who endures is the believer whose patience the Lord values, and the mention is the teaching that the patience is the mark of the prophets. The verse is the record of the three, and the teaching of the patience that the prophets model.",
  "86": "“We admitted them into Our mercy, for they were truly of the righteous.”” completes the mention, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse completes the mention: “We admitted them into Our mercy, for they were truly of the righteous,” or “wa adkhalnahum fee rahmatina innahum mina as-saliheen,” and the commentators note the completion, for the Lord admitted the three into the mercy, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the admission is the reward, and the righteousness is the condition, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the admission is the reward, and the righteousness is the condition, and the verse is the record of the completion, the mercy that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the mercy, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the mercy of the Lord is for the righteous, and the one who lives the righteousness is the one whom the Lord admits, and the believer who hopes the mercy is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the completion of the three is the teaching, the declaration that the righteousness is the path of the mercy, and the believer who reads the completion is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the reward of the righteousness: the three were admitted, and the believer who lives the righteousness is the believer whom the Lord admits, and the completion is the teaching that the righteousness is the path of the mercy. The verse is the record of the completion, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "87": "“And ˹remember˺ when the Man of the Whale stormed off ˹from his city˺ in a rage, thinking We would not restrain him. Then in the ˹veils of˺ darkness he cried out, “There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except You. Glory be to You! I have certainly done wrong.”” records Jonah, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Jonah: “And remember when the Man of the Whale stormed off from his city in a rage, thinking We would not restrain him. Then in the veils of darkness he cried out, ‘There is no god worthy of worship except You. Glory be to You! I have certainly done wrong,’” or “wa tha an-nooni ith thahaba mughadiban fa-zanna an lan naqdira ‘alayhi fa-nada fee az-zulumati an la ilaha illa anta subhanaka innee kuntu mina az-zalimeen,” and the commentators note the story, for Jonah departed in the anger and cried in the darkness, and the verse is the record of the story, the prayer that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the confession, and the verse is the record of the story, the prayer of Jonah. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prayer is the confession, and the darkness is the trial, and the verse is the record of the story, the prayer that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own repentance. The believer is taught that the confession of the wrong is the prayer, and the one who confesses is the one whose prayer the Lord answers, and the believer who repents in the darkness is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the prayer of Jonah is the teaching, the declaration that the confession is the thing that the believer must make, and the believer who reads the prayer is the believer whose repentance the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the repentance: Jonah confessed and was answered, and the believer who repents in the darkness is the believer whose repentance the Lord accepts, and the prayer is the teaching that the confession is the path of the answer. The verse is the record of the prayer, and the teaching of the repentance that the confession requires.",
  "88": "“So We answered his prayer and rescued him from anguish. And so do We save the ˹true˺ believers.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “So We answered his prayer and rescued him from anguish. And so do We save the true believers,” or “fastajabna lahu wa najjaynahu mina al-ghammi wa kathalika nunjee al-mu’mineen,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord answered the prayer and rescued Jonah, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the answer is the mercy, and the law is the promise, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the answer is the mercy, and the rescue is the reward, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the believer is the answered, and the one who calls the Lord is the one whom the Lord rescues, and the believer who hopes the rescue is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the answer to Jonah is the teaching, the declaration that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the prayer: Jonah was rescued, and the believer who calls the Lord is the believer whom the Lord rescues, and the answer is the teaching that the rescue is the thing that the Lord grants. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the hope that the rescue carries.",
  "89": "“And ˹remember˺ when Zachariah cried out to his Lord, “My Lord! Do not leave me childless, though You are the Best of Successors.”” records Zachariah, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Zachariah: “And remember when Zachariah cried out to his Lord, ‘My Lord! Do not leave me childless, though You are the Best of Successors,’” or “wa Zakariyya ith nada rabbahu rabbi la tatharnee fardan wa anta khayru al-waritheen,” and the commentators note the cry, for Zachariah cried the childlessness and the praise, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer that the following verse answers, and the commentators note the significance, for the prayer is the hope, and the praise is the trust, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer of the prophet. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the prayer is the hope, and the praise is the trust, and the verse is the record of the cry, the prayer that the following verse answers.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the prayer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the believer is the thing that the Lord answers, and the one who calls with the praise is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the believer who trusts the Lord is the believer whose trust the Lord values, and the cry of Zachariah is the teaching, the declaration that the praise is the thing that the believer must join to the prayer, and the believer who reads the cry is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the prayer of the hope: Zachariah cried and was answered, and the believer who calls with the praise is the believer whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the cry is the teaching that the praise is the companion of the prayer. The verse is the record of the cry, and the teaching of the hope that the prayer carries.",
  "90": "“So We answered his prayer, granted him John, and made his wife fertile. Indeed, they used to race in doing good, and call upon Us with hope and fear, totally humbling themselves before Us.”” records the answer, and the commentators note the two parts of the answer. The verse records the answer: “So We answered his prayer, granted him John, and made his wife fertile. Indeed, they used to race in doing good, and call upon Us with hope and fear, totally humbling themselves before Us,” or “fastajabna lahu wa wahabna lahu Yahya wa aslahna lahu zawjahu innahum kanoo yusari‘oona fi al-khayrati wa yad‘oonana raghaban wa rahaban wa kanoo lana khashi‘een,” and the commentators note the answer, for the Lord granted John and restored the wife, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy that the following verse parallels, and the commentators note the significance, for the answer is the mercy, and the description is the praise, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy of the Lord. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the answer is the mercy, and the description is the praise, and the verse is the record of the answer, the mercy that the following verse parallels.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the answer, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own hope. The believer is taught that the prayer of the believer is the answered, and the one who races in the good is the one whose hope the Lord fulfills, and the believer who calls with the hope and the fear is the believer whose prayer the Lord accepts, and the answer to Zachariah is the teaching, the declaration that the humility is the thing that the Lord rewards, and the believer who reads the answer is the believer whose hope the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the answer of the prayer: Zachariah was granted, and the believer who races in the good is the believer whose prayer the Lord answers, and the answer is the teaching that the humility is the path of the acceptance. The verse is the record of the answer, and the teaching of the hope that the mercy carries.",
  "91": "“And ˹remember˺ the one who guarded her chastity, so We breathed into her through Our angel, ˹Gabriel,˺ making her and her son a sign for all peoples.”” records Mary, and the commentators note the two parts of the verse. The verse records Mary: “And remember the one who guarded her chastity, so We breathed into her through Our angel, Gabriel, making her and her son a sign for all peoples,” or “wallatee ahsanat farjaha fa-nafakhna feeha min roohina wa ja‘alnaha wabnaha ayatan lil-‘alameen,” and the commentators note Mary, for the verse mentions Mary and the breathing, and the verse is the record of Mary, the mention that closes the series, and the commentators note the significance, for the chastity is the honour, and the sign is the purpose, and the verse is the record of Mary, the closing of the series of the prophets. Ibn Kathir notes the meaning, for the chastity is the honour, and the sign is the purpose, and the verse is the record of Mary, the closing that the series builds to.\n\nThe verse also carries the lesson of the chastity, and the commentators connect it to the believer’s own purity. The believer is taught that the chastity is the honour, and the one who guards the purity is the one whom the Lord honours, and the believer who keeps the purity is the believer whose purity the Lord values, and the mention of Mary is the teaching, the declaration that the chastity is the thing that the Lord honours, and the believer who reads the mention is the believer whose purity the teaching sharpens. Al-Sa’di observes that the verse teaches the believer the honour of the chastity: Mary was honoured, and the believer who guards the purity is the believer whom the Lord honours, and the mention is the teaching that the chastity is the thing that the Lord values. The verse is the record of Mary, and the teaching of the purity that the chastity requires.",
  "92": "\"˹O prophets!˺ Indeed, this religion of yours is ˹only˺ one, and I am your Lord, so worship Me ˹alone˺\" is the verse that gathers the whole chapter together, and it is addressed to all the prophets at once. After Abraham and Lot, Noah, David and Solomon, Job, Jonah, Zachariah, and Mary, the chapter turns to the company itself and says: your religion is one. The word \"religion\" is 'millah,' the way and the creed, and the verse says it is \"˹only˺ one,\" with the emphasis on the oneness. The prophets did not bring different religions, and they did not compete; they carried one message across the ages, and the message is stated in the second half of the verse: \"I am your Lord, so worship Me ˹alone˺.\" The same verse appears elsewhere with one difference: \"Surely this religion of yours is ˹only˺ one, and I am your Lord, so fear Me ˹alone˺\", and the two wordings, worship Me and fear Me, are the two sides of the same command. The object of worship is one, and the response to Him is one.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's central claim, placed exactly in the middle of its stories, and everything before and after it is arranged around it. The stories of the prophets were told to show the one message repeated in one life after another, and the verse now states the message itself, in the voice of God, to the messengers themselves. The verse also answers the pagans of Mecca in the most direct way possible: the prophets they claimed to honour all preached the worship of God alone, and the verse quotes the preaching, so that honouring the prophets and worshipping the idols cannot both be claimed by the same person. The next verse will describe what the followers did with this one religion, and the description is the chapter's saddest line, because the one religion became many sects, and the verse will say where they all return.",
  "93": "\"Yet the people have divided it into sects. But to Us they will all return\" is the verse of the division, and it follows the declaration of oneness like a shadow. The religion was one, and the verse says the people cut it up, and the word behind \"divided\" is 'taqatta'u,' the word for cutting a rope into pieces. The word \"sects\" is the word for parties and factions, and the verse says the followers of the prophets turned the one way into many ways, each group taking its piece and calling it the whole. The other account of the same statement adds the detail that shows the psychology of the division: \"each rejoicing in what they have.\" The sects are not merely different; they are satisfied with their differences, each one certain that its piece of the rope is the rope, and the satisfaction is the seal on the division. The verse is the Quran's diagnosis of the religious fragmentation that the chapter's own audience could see all around them, the Jews and the Christians and the pagans, each claiming the heritage of Abraham and each walking a different road.\n\nThe verse then closes the matter with the sentence that the whole chapter has been preparing: \"But to Us they will all return.\" The word \"all\" gathers every sect, and the return is the Day of Judgment, where the one religion will be sorted from its fragments. The verse is not a call to ignore the differences; it is the announcement that the differences have an expiry date. The factions that divided the truth will be brought back to the One who sent it, and the division will be judged by the original. The verse also carries a warning for every generation of believers: the religion is one, and the cutting of it into sects is a human act, and the return to God is where every human act is measured. The next verse will state what survives the measurement: the good deeds of the believers, recorded and preserved, while everything else is left behind.",
  "94": "\"So whoever does good and is a believer will never be denied ˹the reward for˺ their striving, for We are recording it all\" is the verse of the preserved deeds, and it answers the division of the previous verse with a promise. The sects divided, and the verse does not ask the reader to sort out the sects; it states the two things that will count on the day of return: good deeds and belief. The word \"denied\" is 'kufran,' and the Arabic phrase says their striving will not be met with denial or rejection, and the word \"striving\" is 'sa'y,' the effort itself, so the verse is promising that the effort will be received, not wasted. Then the guarantee: \"for We are recording it all.\" The word \"recording\" is 'katibun,' the word of writing, and the verse says the deeds are being written down, every one of them, by God Himself, and the written record is the reason no effort can be lost. The chapter has spoken of the scales and the mustard seed earlier, and this verse is the same promise in the language of the ledger.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's ground for hope in the middle of its warnings. The division of the religion is real, and the return is certain, and between the two stands this verse: whoever does good as a believer will not lose their effort. The two conditions are both named because they are both needed, the deed and the faith, and the verse places them side by side the way the Quran always does. The verse also teaches the reader where to look when the sects confuse: not at the factions and their claims, but at the two things that will be weighed, the deeds and the belief, and at the One who is recording both. The chapter is about to turn to its final scenes, the Hour and its two destinations, and this verse is the hinge: the record is being written now, and the scenes that follow are where the record is opened.",
  "95": "\"It is impossible for a society which We have destroyed to ever rise again\" opens the final section of the chapter, and the sentence is the closing of a door. The word \"impossible\" is 'haramun,' forbidden, and the verse says it is forbidden for a destroyed society to rise again, and the commentators explain the meaning in two connected ways: the destroyed societies will not return to this world to repent, and they will not return to their former life at all. The door that was open before the destruction is closed after it, and the verse states the closing without exception. The sentence flows directly into the next verse, and the two together form one statement: the destroyed will not rise again, until Gog and Magog break loose, and the meaning will become clear when the two verses are read as one. The verse is the chapter's final warning in the language of its earlier scenes: the societies that were reaped like crops are not coming back, and the time of returning is over for them.\n\nThe verse also carries a comfort inside its severity. The same impossibility that closes the door on the destroyed opens it for the readers, because the readers are still in the time when returning is possible. Every warning in the chapter has been working toward this point: the door is open now, and the verse says it will not stay open. The commentators note that the verse is aimed at the pagans of Mecca, who had seen the ruins of the destroyed nations and still postponed, and the verse tells them what the ruins could not: the postponement has an end, and the end is an impossibility. The next verse will describe the events that surround the end, the breaking loose of Gog and Magog, and the chapter will move from the warning into the description of the Hour itself, the subject that everything in the chapter has been approaching.",
  "96": "\"until ˹after˺ Gog and Magog have broken loose ˹from the barrier˺, swarming down from every hill\" completes the sentence of the previous verse, and it names the great exception that precedes the end. Gog and Magog, 'Yajuj wa Majuj,' are described in the Quran as two corrupting peoples against whom a righteous king built a barrier, and the Quran records their story in the chapter of the Cave: \"O Ⱬul-Qarnain! Surely Gog and Magog are spreading corruption throughout the land. Should we pay you tribute, provided that you build a wall between us and them?\" The barrier held them back, and the verse here says the day will come when they \"break loose\" from it, the word for bursting out of confinement, and the picture that follows is of the release itself: \"swarming down from every hill.\" The word \"swarming\" is 'yansilun,' the pouring down of a flood of people, and \"every hill\" means every direction, the whole horizon filled with the released multitudes. The commentators describe this release as one of the great signs of the approaching Hour, the moment when the last restraints on the world's chaos are removed.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's way of showing that the end of the world is not a single event but a sequence, and that the sequence has already been written. The barrier that holds back corruption is a loan, not a permanent feature of the world, and the verse says the loan will be called in. The same passage continues elsewhere: \"On that Day, We will let them surge ˹like waves˺ over one another. Later, the Trumpet will be blown, and We will gather all ˹people˺ together.\" The release of Gog and Magog is the sign that the trumpet is near, and the next verse will describe what the release ushers in: the True Promise, the Hour itself, arriving while the disbelievers are still busy with their heedlessness. The chapter is moving now at the speed of the events it describes, and each verse is one step closer to the final scene.",
  "97": "ushering in the True Promise. Then—behold!—the disbelievers will stare ˹in horror, crying,˺ \"Oh, woe to us! We have truly been heedless of this. In fact, we have been wrongdoers\" continues the sequence, and the phrase \"the True Promise\" names the Hour by its truest title. The resurrection is the promise, and the word \"true\" is the verse's answer to everyone who called the promise a threat or a tale. The promise arrives, and the arrival is marked with the word \"behold\", the Quran's way of snapping the scene into view, and then the description of the disbelievers: \"will stare ˹in horror, crying,˺\". The Arabic says their eyes will be fixed and wide, the stare of a person whose mind has stopped, and the verse then records their cry: \"Oh, woe to us! We have truly been heedless of this. In fact, we have been wrongdoers.\" The cry has two parts, and both are confessions. They were heedless, and they were wrongdoers, and the verse places the two together because the heedlessness led to the wrongdoing and the wrongdoing fed on the heedlessness.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's gathering of all its earlier scenes into one. The destroyed nations cried the same cry when the punishment arrived, and the mockers of Mecca were told that one breath of the torment would produce the same words, and now the verse records the words of the whole generation of the disbelievers on the Day itself. The chapter has been quoting this cry in advance, verse after verse, and the reader who has followed the chapter knows the words before the verse says them. That familiarity is the chapter's method: the cry is taught in advance so that it can be avoided in time. The verse is the moment the warnings all pointed to, and the stare and the cry are what the heedlessness finally looks like from the inside. The next verses will describe the two destinations that follow the Promise, and the chapter will end with the separation of the two groups, the fuel and the saved.",
  "98": "\"Certainly you ˹disbelievers˺ and whatever you worship instead of Allah will be the fuel of Hell. You are ˹all˺ bound to enter it\" is the verse of the fuel, and it brings the chapter's long argument against the idols to its final image. The worshippers and the worshipped are gathered into one sentence and given one destination: the Fire. The word \"fuel\" is 'hasab,' the word for the wood and brush thrown under a pot, and the verse says the idols and their worshippers will be the fuel of Hell together, and the second sentence makes the destination certain: \"You are ˹all˺ bound to enter it.\" The verse is the chapter's most vivid statement of what the idols are worth: the things that people worshipped instead of God will burn in the same fire as their worshippers, and the burning will be the final demonstration of what they always were. The classical commentators record an important clarification of this verse. When it was revealed, some of the Quraysh debaters asked whether it meant that Jesus and the angels, whom some people worshipped, would also be in the Fire. The answer is given in the verses that follow: those for whom God has destined the finest reward are kept far from Hell. The verse speaks of the idols of stone and the false gods that were willingly worshipped; the honoured servants of God who were wrongly worshipped are not among the fuel.\n\nThe verse also completes the argument that began in the chapter's middle, where Abraham asked what the statues could do. The answer was nothing, and now the verse shows the nothing being carried into the Fire with its worshippers. The verse is harsh, and the harshness is the mercy of the warning, because the same chapter that describes the fuel has spent its length describing the door out of it. The next verse states the logic of the scene in one line, and the line is the last word on the idols: if they had been gods, they would not have entered.",
  "99": "\"Had those idols been ˹true˺ gods, they would not have entered it. And they will be there forever\" is the final verdict on the idols, and it is an argument of two steps. The first step: if the idols had been true gods, they would not have entered the Fire. The verse is not speculating; it is stating the obvious property of divinity. A god is not thrown into Hell, and the beings that will be thrown into Hell are, by that fact alone, not gods. The second step: \"And they will be there forever.\" The word \"forever\" is the word of endless abiding, and the verse says both the idols and their worshippers will remain, and the permanence of their stay is the final measurement of their worth. The things that people served for a lifetime, the stones and the stars and the invented powers, will share their servants' eternity, and the eternity is the Fire. The verse is the chapter's closing argument against every false object of worship, and it needs no further evidence than the scene itself: what enters the Fire was never a god, and what never leaves it was never worth serving.\n\nThe verse also carries the chapter's answer to the question of justice in the Hereafter. The worshippers are not punished alone; the objects of their worship are punished with them, and the pairing is the justice of the scene: the idol that accepted the worship and the worshipper who offered it are both fuel. The verse echoes the warning of Abraham in the middle of the chapter, where he told his people that they and their statues would share a destination, and the echo closes the argument that the chapter has been carrying from its first pages. The next verse describes the state of the fuel in the Fire, and the description is one line, because one line is enough: groaning, and no hearing, and the chapter will then turn to the other group, the ones kept far away.",
  "100": "\"In it they will groan, and will not be able to hear\" is the one-line description of the state of the fuel, and the verse is all the more terrible for its brevity. The word \"groan\" is 'zafir,' the sound of air forced out of a chest in pain, the sound that has no words left in it, and the verse says the groaning is their state. The second half of the verse adds the detail that completes the horror: \"and will not be able to hear.\" The commentators explain this in two ways that meet in the same point. Some say the sound of the Fire itself is so overwhelming that nothing else can be heard, and some say the people of the Fire hear nothing but their own groaning, and either way the verse describes a place where the sense of hearing, the sense that connects a person to others, is useless. The verse is the Quran's way of describing the Fire not through flames but through sound: the groan that never stops, and the hearing that never helps.\n\nThe verse is placed between the verdict on the idols and the description of the saved, and its position is deliberate. The chapter has spent hundreds of words on arguments and stories, and when it finally describes the state of the condemned, it uses one line, because the state itself is the argument's conclusion, and no further words are needed. The verse also carries a warning about what the chapter has called heedlessness: the people who would not hear the warning in this world are described, in the next, as unable to hear anything, and the verse quietly completes the pattern the chapter drew long ago. The ears that were offered the truth and turned away are, in the end, ears without use, and the next verse will turn to the other group, whose hearing is described in the most beautiful contrast: they will not even hear the hissing of the Fire.",
  "101": "\"Surely those for whom We have destined the finest reward will be kept far away from Hell\" turns the chapter to the other group, and the verse begins with the word \"Surely\", the word of certainty. The saved are described by what was destined for them: \"the finest reward\", and the Arabic 'al-husna' means the good and beautiful itself, the reward that is good through and through, and the verse says it was destined for them from Us, the destination preceding the arrival. The second half of the verse describes their position relative to the Fire: \"will be kept far away from Hell\". The word \"far away\" is 'mub'adun,' placed at a distance, and the verse says the distance is not a small one; it is the full removal, the opposite of the nearness the previous verses described. The verse is the first half of the answer to the question about the honoured servants who were wrongly worshipped: these are the ones destined for the finest reward, and they are far from the Fire, and the verse draws the line between the fuel and the saved with the single word \"far away\".\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's definition of salvation, and the definition is worth pausing on. The saved are not described by what they did but by what God destined for them, and the deed and the destiny meet in the earlier verse of the chapter: \"whoever does good and is a believer will never be denied ˹the reward for˺ their striving\". The destiny is the reward of the striving, and the verse keeps the two sides of the matter together: the striving on the human side, and the destined good on the divine side. The verse's position in the chapter is also deliberate: it comes immediately after the description of the fuel, and the two groups are placed side by side so that the reader can see the distance between them. The next verse will describe what the far-away ones experience, and the description begins with their hearing, the sense that the previous verse said was lost to the fuel.",
  "102": "\"not even hearing the slightest hissing from it. And they will delight forever in what their souls desire\" completes the description of the saved, and the first phrase is the exact opposite of the state of the condemned. The previous verses described the people of the Fire groaning and unable to hear, and now the verse says the saved will not even hear \"the slightest hissing\" of the Fire, the word 'hasis' for the faintest sound, the whisper of a flame at a distance. The distance that the previous verse promised is now described through the ear: they are so far from Hell that its sound does not reach them, and the verse chooses the sense of hearing to measure the distance because the ear was the sense the condemned had lost. Then the second half of the verse describes what fills their hearing and their time instead: \"And they will delight forever in what their souls desire.\" The word \"delight\" is 'khalidun,' abiding, and the verse says they will abide in what their souls desire, and the phrase is one of the Quran's broadest promises, because it leaves the desires themselves unnamed. Whatever the soul truly desires, the verse says, is there, and the abiding is forever.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's picture of the reward in its purest form, and it is placed so that the reader can feel the two sides of the final scene at once: the groaning and the delight, the deafness and the hearing, the fuel and the far-away. The same promise appears elsewhere in the Quran with its details: \"Gardens with their Lord under which rivers flow, to stay there forever,\" and the verse here keeps the promise general because the chapter is about to end and the generality is part of the gift. The next verse describes the arrival of the saved on the Day itself, and the arrival is described through the one thing the scene has not yet shown: the greeting.",
  "103": "The Supreme Horror ˹of that Day˺ will not disturb them, and the angels will greet them, ˹saying,˺ \"This is your Day, which you have been promised\" is the verse of the greeting, and it opens with the terror that the saved will not feel. The \"Supreme Horror\" is 'al-faza' al-akbar,' the greatest fright, the overwhelming terror of the Day of Judgment that the Quran describes elsewhere in full, and the verse says it will not disturb the people of the finest reward. The word \"disturb\" is 'yahzunuhum,' the word for grief and distress, and the verse is saying that the great terror of the Day will pass them by, the way a storm passes a sheltered house. Then the greeting: \"the angels will greet them,\" and the greeting is recorded: \"This is your Day, which you have been promised.\" The angels do not bring news; they bring recognition, the words that tell the believers that the day they waited for, the day they were promised through every prophet, has arrived and is theirs. The greeting is the Quran's way of showing that the Day of Judgment is not one day for everyone; it is the day of terror for some and the day of the promise for others, and the verse places the two experiences side by side.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's answer to its own opening. The chapter began with the judgement drawing near and the people turning away in heedlessness, and now, at its end, the same judgement is described as the promised day that the believers are greeted into. The long distance between the opening warning and this greeting is the whole journey of the chapter, and the verse is the arrival. The commentators note the tenderness of the greeting: the angels meet the believers at the gates and tell them the day is theirs, and the words are the fulfillment of the promise that ran through every story the chapter told. The next verse describes the end of the world itself, and the verse after it closes the chapter with the promise of the inheritance, the last word of the book of the prophets.",
  "104": "\"On that Day We will roll up the heavens like a scroll of writings. Just as We produced the first creation, ˹so˺ shall We reproduce it. That is a promise binding on Us. We truly uphold ˹Our promises˺!\" is the verse of the rolling up, and it describes the end of the world with a single, unforgettable image. The heavens will be rolled up \"like a scroll of writings\", the word 'sijill' for the written scroll, and the image is exact: the sky that stretched over every life that ever lived will be folded the way a scribe folds a finished document, because the world's record is complete and the document is being closed. The same image appears elsewhere: \"when on the Day of Judgment the ˹whole˺ earth will be in His Grip, and the heavens will be rolled up in His Right Hand.\" Then the verse states the principle that makes the rolling up possible: \"Just as We produced the first creation, ˹so˺ shall We reproduce it.\" The first creation is the proof of the second, and the verse says the reproduction will be exactly like the production, and the One who began can begin again. The verse then binds the whole matter with the strongest words in the Quran: \"That is a promise binding on Us. We truly uphold ˹Our promises˺!\" The promise is described as an obligation God has placed on Himself, and the verse ends with the declaration that God upholds His promises, the word \"truly\" carrying the full weight of the assurance.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's final statement about the resurrection, and it gathers the chapter's long argument into three movements: the rolling up, the reproduction, and the promise. The same three movements have appeared throughout the chapter in different forms, the scales, the trumpet, the surprise of the Hour, and this verse states them in their final, most confident form. The verse also answers the deepest worry behind every denial of the resurrection: can it really happen? The answer is the beginning of creation itself, the heavens and the earth that exist, and the verse says the One who produced them once will reproduce them, and the promise of it is binding. The chapter has one verse left, and it will close with the promise of the inheritance, the destination of the reproduced world.",
  "105": "Surely, following the ˹heavenly˺ Record, We decreed in the Scriptures: \"My righteous servants shall inherit the land\" is the last verse of the chapter, and it closes the book of the prophets with a promise about the end of the story. The verse says the decree was written, and the commentators explain that this promise appears in the Psalms of David, the Scripture given to the prophet whose story the chapter told, and the Quran is confirming the decree that the earlier Scriptures carried: \"My righteous servants shall inherit the land.\" The word \"inherit\" is the word of receiving what was another's, and the verse says the righteous will receive the land, and the commentators explain the land in two connected ways: the earth at the end of time, when the righteous inherit the world after the wrongdoers, and the land of Paradise, the final home that the righteous enter. Both meanings meet, because the inheritance of the earth and the inheritance of Paradise are the two installments of the same promise, the promise that the earth belongs, in the end, to the people of God.\n\nThe verse is the chapter's answer to the mockers of Mecca, who owned the land and the Kaaba and thought their ownership was the verdict. The verse says ownership is temporary, and the inheritance is decreed, and the decree was written before their pride began. The chapter that opened with the judgement drawing near now closes with the inheritance, and the two ends of the chapter hold its whole message: the account is coming, and the righteous will inherit. The word \"righteous\" gathers all the names the chapter has celebrated, Abraham and Noah and Lot and David and Job and the rest, and extends the promise to every reader who joins their company. The chapter of the prophets ends not with a warning but with a promise, and the promise is the oldest one in the book: the land belongs to the righteous, and the decree is already written, and the One who wrote it upholds what He writes.",
  "106": "\"Surely this ˹Quran˺ is sufficient ˹as a reminder˺ for those devoted to worship\" closes the main body of the chapter with a claim about the Book itself, and the claim is an answer to every demand for more. The word \"sufficient\" translates the Arabic 'balagh,' the word for something that reaches its goal and delivers its message completely, and the verse says this Quran is that. The people who asked for signs, who wanted a prophet of a different kind, and who kept turning away, are told that what they already have is enough. The Book is not one proof among many; it is the delivery itself, the reminder that reaches the heart it is meant to reach. The same claim appears elsewhere: \"Is it not enough for them that We have sent down to you the Book, ˹which is˺ recited to them. Surely in this ˹Quran˺ is a mercy and reminder for people who believe.\" The sufficiency is stated twice in the Quran, because the demand for more is the oldest human excuse, and the answer to it never changes: you have been given enough.\n\nThe verse then names the people for whom the Book is sufficient: \"those devoted to worship.\" The Arabic is 'qawmin abidin,' a people of worship, and the word matters. The Quran is sufficient for the one who worships, because worship is what opens the heart to the reminder. The same Book that is enough for the worshipper is not enough for the mocker, not because the Book falls short but because the mocker never opens it with the heart it requires. The verse is the chapter's final word on its own audience: the stories, the arguments, and the warnings of Al-Anbya were all delivered, and they were enough, and the only question left is whether the reader belongs to the people of worship. The chapter closes with two verses of the Prophet's own message, and this verse is the threshold: the Book is sufficient, for those devoted to worship.",
  "107": "\"We have sent you ˹O Prophet˺ only as a mercy for the whole world\" is one of the most famous verses in the Quran, and its fame is earned, because the whole mission of the final Prophet is summarized in it. The word \"mercy\" is 'rahmah,' and the verse says the sending of Muhammad was not a punishment, not a burden, and not a favour for one nation; it was a mercy, and the mercy is described as \"for the whole world\", the word 'alamin' covering all peoples and all creation. The verse then has a quiet precision in its word order: \"only\" comes after \"We have sent you\", so the sentence reads that the sending itself is only mercy, and nothing else. Every aspect of the mission, the warnings included, is a mercy, because a warning is a mercy to the one who hears it in time, and even the punishment announced is the mercy of a truth told in advance. The same message is stated elsewhere with its twin: \"We have sent you ˹O Prophet˺ only as a deliverer of good news and a warner to all of humanity, but most people do not know.\"\n\nThe verse is also the chapter's final answer to the mockers who treated the Prophet as an enemy. He was sent to them, and the sending was mercy, and their rejection of him was a rejection of the mercy itself. The commentators note that the verse describes the Prophet's mission in its widest frame: he came for the people of his time and for every people after them, and the mercy of his message, the Quran, is the mercy that the chapter has been describing from its first verse. The chapter of the prophets ends by telling the reader what all the prophets pointed to: the final messenger, and the mercy he carried. The next verse records the heart of what he was told to say, and the heart of the mercy is one sentence.",
  "108": "Say, \"What has been revealed to me is this: ‘Your God is only One God.’ Will you then submit?\" records the message the Prophet was commanded to deliver, and the verse places the whole of his preaching in a single sentence. The word \"revealed\" is the key: the Prophet is told to announce that what he carries is revelation, not his own invention, and the content of the revelation is stated in full: \"Your God is only One God.\" The word \"only\" closes every door to partnership, and the sentence is the creed the chapter has spent a hundred verses building. Then the verse turns the message into a question: \"Will you then submit?\" The word \"submit\" is the word of Islam itself, the surrender of the whole self to God, and the question is the invitation that follows every statement of the truth. The verse does not end in a declaration; it ends in a question, because the truth has been stated and the response now belongs to the hearer. The same command appears elsewhere, where the Prophet is told to say that \"I am only a man like you\" and that \"it has been revealed to me that your God is only One God\", and to take the Straight Way towards Him.\"\n\nThe verse is the chapter's version of the call that every prophet delivered, the same call the chapter quoted earlier: \"There is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Me, so worship Me ˹alone˺.\" The wording changes slightly, but the content is one, and the verse places the final statement of the chapter in the same chain. The question \"Will you then submit?\" is also the question the whole chapter has been asking in different forms, and the next verse records what the Prophet is to say if the answer is no. The mercy of verse 107 and the message of verse 108 stand together: the mercy was the sending, and the message is the oneness, and the submission is the door.",
  "109": "If they turn away, then say, \"I have warned you all equally. I do not know if what you are threatened with is near or far\" gives the Prophet his instruction for the case of rejection, and the instruction has two parts. The first is the declaration of fairness: \"I have warned you all equally.\" The Arabic phrase means the warning was delivered to everyone on the same terms, with nothing held back and no one favored, and the verse says it plainly so that no one on the Day can claim they were not told. The second part is the confession of a limit: \"I do not know if what you are threatened with is near or far.\" The Prophet is told to say that the timing of the punishment is not his knowledge, and the confession is the Quran's way of drawing the line between the messenger and his Lord. The messenger delivers, and the timing belongs to God, and the verse shows the Prophet standing honestly at that line instead of pretending to know what he does not. The same answer is given elsewhere when people asked about the Hour: \"That knowledge is only with my Lord. He alone will reveal it when the time comes\".\n\nThe verse is also a teaching about the manners of the caller. The one who warns does not add to the warning his own guesses, and he does not soften it to please the hearers, and he does not pretend to control its timing. He warns equally, he admits what he does not know, and he leaves the matter to God. The verse is the Prophet's own posture in the face of rejection, and the chapter gives it to every believer who carries the message after him: the delivery is yours, and the timing is God's, and the one who delivered honestly has done his part. The next verse explains why the warning can be left to God, and the reason is God's knowledge of everything that is said and hidden.",
  "110": "\"Allah surely knows what you say openly and whatever you hide\" continues the Prophet's words, and the verse is the reason behind the calm of the previous one. The warning has been delivered, and the timing has been left to God, and the verse says why the delivery can be trusted to Him: He knows what is spoken aloud and what is kept hidden. The two halves of the sentence cover the whole of human speech. The open words are the arguments, the mockery, and the public denial, and the hidden words are the whispers, the private counsels, and the thoughts that never reach the tongue, and the verse says God knows both. The word \"surely\" gives the statement the force of an oath, and the verse is the Quran's answer to the secret conversations that the chapter recorded in its opening: \"The evildoers would converse secretly\". The whisperers of the chapter's first verses are now told, at the chapter's end, that their whispers were never private, and the verse closes the circle the chapter drew at its beginning.\n\nThe verse is also a comfort and a warning in one. It comforts the Prophet, whose enemies plotted against him in secret, because the plotting is known to God and answered by God. And it warns the plotters, because nothing they say in the dark is dark to the One who hears everything. The verse teaches the believer the habit of living as if every word were heard, because it is, and the habit is the beginning of the reverence the chapter has been describing. The next verse completes the Prophet's speech with the confession of his own limit, and the two verses together show the complete posture of the messenger: the message delivered, the knowledge of God affirmed, and the limits of the servant admitted.",
  "111": "\"I do not know if this ˹delay˺ is possibly a test for you and an enjoyment for a while\" is the second confession of the Prophet's limits, and it explains the delay that the mockers had used as their argument. The disbelievers had asked when the punishment would come, and they had treated the delay as proof that it never would, and the verse gives the true explanation of the delay in two words: \"a test\" and \"an enjoyment\". The word \"test\" is 'fitnah,' the trial that exposes what is in the heart, and the verse says the delay may be that: a span of time in which each person shows what they will do with the warning they have received. The word \"enjoyment\" is 'mata',' the provision of a limited term, and the verse says the delay may be that as well: a brief enjoyment that ends. Both readings meet in the same point: the delay is not a cancellation. It is a period with a purpose and an end, and the verse says the Prophet does not know which reading applies, because the knowledge of it is God's.\n\nThe verse is the Quran's answer to the oldest objection against its warnings, the objection from silence. The wrongdoer sins, and nothing happens, and the silence feels like permission, and the verse says the silence is a test or a temporary enjoyment, never a verdict of innocence. The same explanation appears throughout the Quran, and this chapter has stated it before: \"In fact, We have allowed enjoyment for these ˹Meccans˺ and their forefathers for such a long time ˹that they took it for granted˺.\" The verse also teaches the believer not to read God's timing with human impatience. The delay that looks like escape is often the test itself, and the one who is wise reads the delay as the question it is, not as the answer it appears to be. The next verse closes the chapter with the Prophet's prayer, and the prayer is the natural ending of a message that has said everything it came to say.",
  "112": "˹In the end,˺ the Prophet said, \"My Lord! Judge ˹between us˺ in truth. And our Lord is the Most Compassionate, Whose help is sought against what you claim\" closes the chapter with a prayer, and the chapter of the prophets ends the way it should: by handing the matter to God. The word \"Judge\" is 'ifta'h,' which means to open and to decide, and the commentators explain that the Prophet is asking God to open the matter between him and his people, to decide it, and to settle it, and the prayer asks for the judgement \"in truth\", because God's judgement is the only one that ends a dispute rather than prolonging it. The same prayer appears in the story of Shu'aib's people, whose words the Quran records: \"Our Lord! Judge between us and our people with truth. You are the best of those who judge.\" The Prophet of this chapter joins the same prayer, and the chapter that began with the judgement drawing near ends with the Prophet asking for it.\n\nThe second half of the verse turns the prayer into an appeal: \"our Lord is the Most Compassionate, Whose help is sought against what you claim.\" The claims are the accusations of the mockers, the \"confused dreams\" and the claim that \"he has fabricated it\", and the rest, and the verse says the help against them is sought from the Most Compassionate, the name of God that the mockers themselves refused to hear, as the chapter recorded: \"while they disbelieve at the mention of the Most Compassionate.\" The verse quietly closes that theme too: the name they rejected is the name the Prophet calls upon, and the mercy they denied is the help he seeks. The chapter ends with the dispute handed to God, the appeal made in the name of His mercy, and the reader left on the threshold: the judgement the chapter announced in its first line has drawn nearer with every verse since."
};
